,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T14:07:24Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/4a5b20b3aad8f9a546ad1bbf6336b797c10b5489","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKDRhNWIyMGIzYWFkOGY5YTU0NmFkMWJiZjYzMzZiNzk3YzEwYjU0ODk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002","sha":"b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002"}],"sha":"4a5b20b3aad8f9a546ad1bbf6336b797c10b5489","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/4a5b20b3aad8f9a546ad1bbf6336b797c10b5489"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1272922?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Mtze","id":1272922,"login":"Mtze","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyNzI5MjI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mtze","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:06:51Z","email":"github@linhuber.org","name":"Matthias Linhuber"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:06:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"New Phase: Infrastructure Setup Phase (#1593)\n\n* Init\n\n* Add setup config and auth-aware infrastructure provisioning\n\n* Fix infrastructure setup CI issues\n\n* Add infrastructure setup components and update environment configurations\n\n* feat: add infrastructure setup interfaces and network mutations for resource and provider configurations\n\n- Introduced interfaces for provider configuration, resource configuration, resource instance, and setup configuration.\n- Implemented network mutations for creating, updating, deleting, and validating resource and provider configurations.\n- Added queries to fetch instances, provider configurations, and resource configurations.\n- Enhanced ExecutionPage and ProvidersPage to utilize new network queries and mutations.\n- Updated ResourceConfigPage and SetupConfigPage to manage resource and setup configurations with improved UI components.\n\n* Refactor resource instance management and enhance provider configuration handling\n\n- Added new SQL queries for counting non-terminal instances, creating, deleting, and retrieving resource instances.\n- Updated the CreateResourceInstance and DeleteResourceInstance functions to use structured parameters.\n- Refactored the service layer to handle provider configuration deletion and validation more effectively.\n- Enhanced the resource configuration service to support deletion and retrieval using structured parameters.\n- Updated SQLC configuration to emit JSON tags in camel case and support nullable UUIDs.\n- Improved error handling in service methods to ensure robustness during instance creation and retries.\n\n* feat: enhance infrastructure setup with new API endpoints and documentation\n\n* feat: add comprehensive tests for infrastructure setup, provider configurations, and resource management\n\n* feat: Refactor infrastructure setup API with DTOs and validation\n\n- Introduced DTOs for phase and provider configurations to streamline API responses and requests.\n- Implemented validation logic for upsert requests in both phase and provider configurations.\n- Updated API routes to utilize new DTO structures for improved clarity and maintainability.\n- Changed server host from localhost:8089 to localhost:8091 across documentation and code.\n- Enhanced error handling in API responses with retry options for loading configurations.\n- Added validation for resource configuration creation and updates, ensuring required fields and formats.\n- Refactored service methods to return DTOs instead of raw database models for better abstraction.\n\n* refactor: streamline error handling in ExecutionPage, ProvidersPage, and SetupConfigPage\n\n* refactor: simplify name template validation by removing template parsing\n\n* refactor: source teams via phase configurator instead of manual import\n\nMove the Infrastructure Setup phase''s team/student sourcing from a\nmanual sibling-phase picker to the standard PROMPT phase-IO mechanism,\nmirroring the Assessment phase. The phase configurator now wires the\nteams and teamAllocation inputs; core''s dependency graph drives\nresolution.\n\n- Declare `teams` (phase-level) and `teamAllocation` (participation-level)\n  required inputs in core''s initInfrastructureSetup.\n- Rewrite execution/target_resolver to call FetchAndMerge* with this\n  phase''s own coursePhaseID, with local parseTeams/parsePersons helpers\n  modeled on the Assessment phase.\n- Drop team_source_course_phase_id and student_source_course_phase_id\n  from course_phase_config (schema, queries, regenerated sqlc).\n- Strip the same fields from the upsert DTO, service, validation, and\n  tests.\n- Drop the sourcePhase flag from ConfigHandler; report semesterTag,\n  providerConfig, and resourceConfig readiness instead.\n- Register /info in main.go so the management console''s System Status\n  page reports the service as healthy.\n- Simplify SetupConfigPage to only manage the semester tag and point\n  lecturers at the phase configurator for input wiring.\n- Refresh Swagger docs to match the new DTO shapes and descriptions.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: address CodeQL allocation overflow + gitignore the new db volume\n\n- Drop the len(team.Members)+len(team.Tutors) sum from the make() capacity\n  hint that CodeQL flagged as potentially overflowing. Use len(team.Members)\n  alone; tutor appends grow the slice dynamically. The over-allocation is\n  negligible for the team sizes this code handles.\n- Add postgres_infrastructure_setup_data/ to .gitignore so the docker\n  volume directory does not appear in git status.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore: regenerate core sqlc output after main merge\n\nThe merge with main reordered Test*TypeExists functions in the\ngenerated sqlc Go code; CI''s \"Check sqlc generated files are current\"\nstep fails until the file is regenerated and committed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix biome lint and wire phase into CI matrices\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Simplify provider and page code without behavior change\n\n- Collapse rancher lookupUserByEmail into a single struct and index check\n- Extract keycloak group lookup into a shared searchGroupByName helper\n- Drop dead no-auth RetryFailedInstance wrapper and unused CopyService conn field\n- Reuse shared LoadingPage/ErrorPage on the resource config page\n- Inline the StatusBadge label and remove the unused getResourceConfig query\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Regenerate API spec after DTO package rename\n\n* Self Team Allocation: Revert unrelated timeframe timezone change\n\nThe timeframe column is TIMESTAMP without zone, so pgtype.Timestamp.Time is\nalready UTC-located and the original instant comparison was correct.\nRebuilding both bounds in time.Local shifted the survey window by the host\noffset when running the server outside Docker.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix environment, routing and startup blockers\n\n- .env.template: drop the alias block that redefined DB_HOST_/DB_PORT_ for team\n  allocation, self team allocation, assessment, interview and certificate from\n  variables that do not exist. Those assignments came after the real definitions\n  and emptied the database configuration of five running services.\n- Register the SDK phase-config endpoint on the phase-scoped group. It is mounted\n  as a bare /config, so on the global group it resolved without :coursePhaseID and\n  returned 500 on every call while /info advertised the capability.\n- Restrict the phase routes to admins and lecturers. These carry external provider\n  credentials, and the client already gated its pages the same way, so an editor\n  could only reach them by calling the API directly.\n- Wait for Keycloak before starting: the server initialises the auth middleware\n  through synchronous OIDC discovery and exits when it is unavailable.\n- Read INFRASTRUCTURE_SETUP_HOST from window.env. It is not part of the shared\n  state EnvType, so the normalised env object dropped it and the base URL fell\n  back to the page origin, which has no proxy in local development.\n- Add INFRASTRUCTURE_SETUP_HOST and the Sentry DSN to the templates and compose\n  files, and the local database overrides to .env.dev.template.\n- Validate ENCRYPTION_KEY at startup instead of on the first credential write.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Make execution safe to run twice and report partial results\n\nProvisioning could run twice over the same instance and silently create\nresources nobody was added to.\n\n- Claim pending instances in a single UPDATE ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED instead\n  of listing them and marking them in_progress afterwards, so two overlapping\n  runs cannot pick up the same row.\n- Guard the trigger with a transaction-scoped advisory lock around the\n  non-terminal count and the inserts. Counting first and inserting after left a\n  window where two callers both saw an idle phase. The second caller now gets 409.\n  Targets are resolved before the transaction because that calls core over HTTP.\n- Refuse to trigger a phase whose providers have no credentials, which is the\n  state a copied phase starts in. Previously every instance was created and then\n  failed one by one on decryption.\n- Replace the catch-all status update with MarkInstanceCreated, MarkInstancePartial\n  and MarkInstanceFailed. The old query wrote all four columns unconditionally, so\n  a failure blanked an external ID recorded by an earlier success.\n- Report members that could not be granted access as warnings on the resource and\n  record the instance as partial. GitLab and Rancher used to fail the whole\n  instance on the first bad member; Keycloak, Slack and Outline skipped them and\n  reported success, leaving an empty group with nothing to show the lecturer.\n  Partial instances can be retried and heal once the users exist upstream.\n- Retry now returns the row it reset, so retrying an unknown instance is a 404 and\n  retrying one that is not failed or partial is a 409. It previously reported 202\n  and started a worker in both cases.\n- Resolve targets once per scope per run rather than once per instance, which\n  issued two core requests for every resource.\n- Drop DeleteResource from the provider interface. It had no caller, and since\n  providers adopt resources by name it could have removed something the course\n  does not own. Instance deletion stays a PROMPT-only operation, as the dialog\n  already stated.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Make a copied phase report and behave as unconfigured\n\nA copied phase kept its provider rows but not the credentials, and still reported\nitself as fully configured. The lecturer saw a ready phase, triggered execution,\nand every instance failed on decryption.\n\n- Copy the course_phase_config row as well. HandlePhaseConfig returns all-false\n  as soon as that row is missing, so the semester tag and everything after it were\n  reported as unconfigured no matter what else had been copied.\n- Run the whole copy in one transaction so a failure part-way cannot leave a\n  half-copied phase, and skip resource configs that already exist on the target so\n  repeating a copy does not duplicate them.\n- Report only providers holding credentials as configured, expose that as a\n  ''configured'' flag on the API, and show ''credentials required'' on the provider card.\n- Refuse to create or update a resource config whose provider has no credentials,\n  and leave such providers out of the resource config picker.\n- Validate the resource type against the provider that has to create it. It was\n  free text passed to providers that never read it, so the field implied a choice\n  that had no effect; the dialog now offers only the supported kinds.\n- Answer 400 rather than 500 when a resource config request is rejected.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the provider HTTP integrations\n\nEach provider had a defect that only shows against a real API.\n\nGitLab\n- Search the configured parent''s subgroups and compare the path exactly. Matching\n  a full_path suffix across every visible group also matched another course''s\n  group of the same name, adding the students to it.\n- Drop min_access_level=50 from the group search. A maintainer token never saw the\n  groups it had created, recreated them, and failed on the taken path.\n- Invite members through /invitations instead of resolving a user ID first.\n  /users?search= only returns the email field to admin tokens, so member adds\n  failed after the group had already been created. Invitations also work for\n  students who have not signed in yet.\n\nRancher\n- Resolve users through the principals search endpoint and confirm the hit against\n  the requested address. The v3 user schema has no email field, so ?email= was not\n  a filter at all and Rancher returned an unfiltered list whose first entry, the\n  local admin, was bound into the student project.\n- Use the principal ID Rancher returns rather than assembling local://<id>, which\n  is wrong on any LDAP- or OIDC-backed instance.\n\nSlack\n- Form-encode requests. conversations.list and users.lookupByEmail are documented\n  as form-only and reject a JSON body.\n- Declare the pagination response inside the loop. A page without response_metadata\n  left the previous cursor in place, so the same page was requested until the\n  30-minute context expired.\n- Stop excluding archived channels when resolving a name conflict. The archived\n  channel owns the name, so skipping it turned name_taken into a permanent\n  ''channel not found''.\n\nOutline\n- Check ok on the membership call, which was ignored, and use collections.add_user.\n- Paginate collections.list; a collection past the first page was never found and\n  a duplicate was created under the same name.\n\nAll four are covered by httptest servers. Slack gained an overridable base URL so\nits API calls can be exercised at all.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Reject name templates and credentials that cannot work\n\n- ResolveName now fails on a placeholder it does not know instead of leaving the\n  raw {{...}} in the name of a real external resource, and resource config\n  validation runs the same check so a typo is caught when the template is saved\n  rather than when provisioning runs.\n- Remove {{.CourseName}}, {{.TeamIndex}} and {{.Year}}. They were documented but\n  never populated by either resolver, so they always rendered as an empty string\n  or 0. The semester tag keeps all three of its spellings, since saved templates\n  may use any of them.\n- Require credential values to be strings and reject unknown fields. A nested\n  object was previously encrypted without complaint and only surfaced much later,\n  as a 500 when the worker tried to build the provider.\n- Answer 400 rather than 500 when provider credentials are rejected.\n- Seed the semester tag field only when the config loads. Refilling it whenever it\n  was empty meant an intentionally cleared tag could never be saved.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Cover the execution worker and target resolver with tests\n\nThe worker and the target resolver had no tests at all, though they hold the retry,\nconcurrency and status logic.\n\n- worker: an instance reaches created, a member warning yields partial with the\n  external ID intact, retries stop at the limit and mark the instance failed, a\n  transient failure still succeeds, a vanished target and an unresolvable name\n  template fail without calling the provider, and a crash-interrupted instance is\n  recovered at startup.\n- Two workers racing on the same phase call the provider exactly once, which is the\n  regression the atomic claim exists to prevent.\n- target resolver: parseTeams and parsePersons skip malformed upstream entries and\n  only reject a payload that is not a list; targetIndex resolves both scopes.\n- provider config: non-string and unknown credential fields are rejected, and every\n  provider reports its supported resource types.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Align tooling with the other phases and rewrite the guide\n\n- Move the component to rspack 2.1 and yarn 4.18, matching core and every other\n  component. It was a major version behind, so the Module Federation runtime in the\n  remote did not match the host, and the lockfile carried two rspack trees with all\n  their platform bindings. Also rename the cache type to the 2.x spelling and use\n  node: import specifiers, leaving the config identical to the certificate phase''s\n  apart from the component name and port.\n- Bump prompt-sdk to v1.2.3, the version the other phase services use.\n- Register the service with the swagger pre-commit hook. CI already checked the spec\n  was current, so an annotation change would have failed there instead.\n- Add the phase to the AGENTS.md inventory, the glossary and the stress-test service\n  list, and pin its database to the same postgres patch as the rest.\n- Rewrite docs/contributor/infrastructure-setup-phase.md. It documented a port, a\n  file layout, an API surface and a test suite that never existed. It now describes\n  the real routes and roles, the partial status, the concurrency guarantees, why\n  external resources are never deleted, and what copying a phase does.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Add end-to-end coverage\n\n- Wire the phase into the e2e stack: its own server, remote and database in\n  docker-compose.e2e.yml, the /infrastructure-setup proxy locations that Traefik\n  provides in prod, the host and database variables, a fixed test encryption key,\n  and a readiness poll in global-setup.\n- Seed a standalone phase on the full course with two participants, so a\n  per_student resource config resolves to real provisioning targets.\n- mf-smoke.spec.ts proves the remote loads and renders inside the core shell,\n  which is the failure mode the window.env host fix addresses.\n- lecturer-journey.spec.ts drives the phase through its own API: an unconfigured\n  phase reports nothing ready, a resource config is refused before its provider\n  has credentials, credentials never come back over the wire, unknown placeholders\n  and unsupported resource types are rejected, and a second trigger while work is\n  in flight returns 409 without creating a second set of instances. The provider\n  points at a blackholed TEST-NET address so the instances stay non-terminal and\n  that conflict is deterministic rather than a race against the worker.\n- Both specs reset the phase before and after they run, so a rerun against a live\n  stack starts from the same state.\n- Return [] rather than null from the instances endpoint when nothing is\n  provisioned, which the empty-phase assertions surfaced.\n\n* Self Team Allocation: Restore the timeframe timezone handling\n\nReverts 1303907. Removing that hunk was wrong: TestAssignTeamRoute,\nTestAssignTeamRouteTeamFull and TestLeaveTeamRoute fail without it on any host\nthat is not on UTC.\n\nThe timeframe column is TIMESTAMP without a zone and the value is written from a\nlocal time.Time, so the stored digits are local wall-clock and reading them back\nyields a UTC-located time carrying those digits. Comparing that against\ntime.Now() is therefore off by the host offset, which is why the tests pass in\nCI and fail on a UTC+2 machine. Rebuilding both bounds in time.Local restores\nthe intended instant.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Adopt SDK startup helpers in the phase service\n\nReplace the service-local initKeycloak and runMigrations with\npromptSDK.InitPhaseKeycloak and sdkUtils.RunMigrations, matching the\nconvention the other phase services now follow, and bump prompt-sdk to\nv1.3.0 where those helpers live.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Copy the phase component manifest in the clients base image\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the provider defects that break provisioning\n\nGitLab:\n\n- parent_group_id could not be used at all. It was declared as *int, but credential\n  values are validated and stored as strings, so decoding a configured value failed\n  with \"cannot unmarshal string into Go struct field parent_group_id of type int\" and\n  every GitLab operation errored out. It is now a string parsed on use, and a\n  non-numeric value is rejected when the credentials are validated rather than on the\n  first provisioning run.\n- The group search is paginated and re-resolves after a failed create. Search is a\n  substring match, so \"ios-team-1\" also matches \"ios-team-10\"; with enough sibling\n  groups the exact match fell outside the first page, the provider concluded \"absent\",\n  and the create then failed on a taken path.\n- An unmapped role or an unrecognized permission string silently became Guest. A\n  GitLab Guest cannot read code in a private project, so the team was reported as\n  provisioned while having no repository access. Both are warnings now, which makes\n  the instance partial and retryable.\n\nKeycloak:\n\n- A create losing a race answered 409 and failed the instance through all three\n  retries. The conflict now re-resolves the group.\n- The group search asks for exact=true and pages, for the same substring-match reason\n  as GitLab.\n- The reported URL pointed at the admin REST endpoint, which answers 401 JSON when the\n  link is opened. It now points at the admin console.\n- Added the mocked-HTTP coverage the provider had none of: create, adoption, exact\n  search, conflict recovery, and the unknown-member warning path.\n\nOutline:\n\n- users.list never checked ok. A workspace-wide permission or rate-limit failure\n  returns HTTP 200 with ok=false and no data, which was reported as \"outline user not\n  found for email\", blaming the student for a token problem.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Add the identity and safety plumbing the providers need\n\nGroundwork for per-team GitLab projects and the Outline collection binding. No provider\nbehaviour changes yet.\n\nStable identity. `CreateResourceInput` carries a `StableKey`\n(`prompt:<coursePhaseID>:<resourceConfigID>:<targetID>`) and the instance''s\n`ExistingExternalID`. A provider can now pair an external object with PROMPT and prove\nownership before adopting a resource, and a retried instance re-attaches by ID instead of\nlooking the resource up by name. Both are derived from data the instance already has, so\nthere is no migration.\n\nTemplated extra config. A provider declares which extra-config keys are name templates\nthrough `TemplatedExtraConfigKeys`. Only those are resolved, and they are validated with\nthe name-template rules on create and update, so an unresolvable placeholder is rejected\nat save time rather than reaching a real resource name mid-run. Every other extra-config\nvalue stays literal, so Rancher''s `roleTemplateId` is untouched and a setting can still\ncontain braces.\n\nConfig edit lock. Editing a config that already has a non-failed instance is refused.\nThe partial unique indexes on `resource_instance` mean a live instance blocks a second\none for the same target, so an edited config never re-provisions: the row would describe\none resource while a differently named one existed externally, invisible from the UI. A\npermission-mapping edit is refused for the same reason, since it cannot reach memberships\nthat were already granted.\n\nError sanitisation. Providers formatted the raw upstream response body into their errors,\nand the worker persists that string in `resource_instance.error_message`, which the UI\nrenders. Bodies can carry tokens, internal hostnames or other users'' data. All eleven\ncall sites now go through `provider.HTTPError`, which reports method, path and status\nonly, and logs a truncated body at debug level.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Provision GitLab projects, not just groups\n\nThe phase could only create GitLab groups, so a team got an empty subgroup and every\nrepository was made by hand. `CreateResource` also never read `input.ResourceType`: it\ncreated a group whatever was asked for, which meant a config row carrying a resource type\nthe API validation had not seen would create a group named after the project template and\nreport it as a success.\n\nGitLab now supports `group` and `project`, `CreateResource` dispatches on the resource\ntype, and an unrecognised type is refused rather than falling through to group creation.\n\nA project resource creates the team''s subgroup and the project inside it. The subgroup is\nan explicit side effect rather than a tracked dependency: instances carry no ordering, so\na project cannot wait for a separately configured group row. Both paths adopt by exact\npath under the same parent, so a project config and a group config for the same team\nconverge on one subgroup instead of racing.\n\nA project requires both the provider''s `parent_group_id` and a `parent_group_template`\nresolving to a team subgroup. Without them the project would land in the token owner''s\npersonal namespace, or need a top-level group most instances forbid, so both are refused\nrather than guessed.\n\nMembers are granted access on the subgroup only. GitLab group members inherit access to\nevery project in the group, so per-project invitations would add nothing, and a test\nasserts none are sent.\n\nExistence is decided by an exact lookup on the URL-encoded namespaced path, not by\nparsing the create error. GitLab''s duplicate-path response is the least specified part of\nthat surface: create answers 400 with a field-keyed message map while the fork endpoint\nanswers 409 with a flat array of sentences. The create error is only a signal to re-run\nthe exact lookup, which also covers losing a race.\n\nVisibility is sent explicitly and defaults to private. Omitting it falls back to the\ninstance-wide `default_project_visibility`, which an administrator may have set to\npublic, so a team repository could have been created world-readable.\n`initialize_with_readme` defaults to true so the repository has a branch to clone.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Bind an Outline group to each team''s collection\n\nA collection was created with `permission: \"read\"`, which in Outline means every member\nof the workspace can read it: `Collection.isPrivate` is false whenever a permission is\nset, and the read policy then returns true without checking any membership. Every team''s\ncollection was readable by every student in the workspace, and the per-user memberships\nadded afterwards granted nothing beyond that.\n\nCollections are now created private, by omitting `permission`, and access is granted\nthrough a group bound to the collection.\n\nWhat this does, and what it cannot do. Outline has no OIDC group synchronisation: its\nOIDC plugin reads no groups claim, so collection visibility can never be decided from a\ntoken. Keycloak authenticates the user; Outline authorises against its own group\nmembership. When both are configured, PROMPT puts the same snapshot of team members into\nthe Keycloak group and into the Outline group, and binds the Outline group to the\ncollection. Nothing is synchronised afterwards and no membership is ever removed, which\nis why teams must be settled before provisioning runs.\n\nThe group is keyed on Outline''s `externalId`, using the instance''s stable key rather than\na display name, so a renamed collection or an edited template still resolves to the same\ngroup.\n\nCollections are no longer adopted by name alone. A created collection carries an\nownership marker in its description, and a same-named collection without that marker is\nrefused with an actionable error. Outline collections share one flat workspace namespace,\nso name adoption could hand a team read access to an unrelated collection. A retried\ninstance re-attaches by its recorded ID, so the name decides nothing on the path that\nmatters.\n\nMembers whose roles map to different permissions get one group each. A group carries a\nsingle collection permission, so picking one for a team of students plus a tutor would\nsilently over- or under-grant. Split groups get a distinguishable display name.\n`collections.add_group` always sends the permission explicitly, because Outline defaults\nthat call to read_write.\n\nOnce the collection exists every later step contributes warnings instead of failing, so\nthe instance stays partial and retryable with its external ID intact. Member lookup\nresolves all emails in one or two calls instead of paging the workspace once per member.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Document what the phase provisions and what it cannot\n\nFrontend:\n\n- The resource-type dropdown already rendered every kind the server reports, so GitLab''s\n  new `project` needed no change; only the \"providers support exactly one kind\" comment\n  was stale.\n- The extra-config field explains that most values are literal and lists the two keys\n  that are name templates, per provider and resource type.\n- The Resource Configurations page states how Outline access actually works whenever an\n  Outline config exists, and points out an Outline config that has no Keycloak group\n  config at the same scope. The limitation belongs where a lecturer is making the\n  decision, not only in the contributor docs.\n\nDocs:\n\n- GitLab now lists `group` and `project`, with the iPraktikum layout and the reason a\n  project requires both a parent group ID and a parent group template.\n- A new Outline access section says plainly that Outline has no OIDC group\n  synchronisation and cannot decide collection visibility from a token: Keycloak\n  authenticates, Outline authorises from its own group membership, and PROMPT provisions\n  the same member snapshot into both groups when both are configured.\n- Documented that memberships are never removed, so teams must be settled before\n  provisioning, and that a config is frozen once it has provisioned.\n- Added a Known limitations section collecting these, including that Slack, Outline and\n  Rancher are covered only by mocked tests against the published contracts.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Verify provisioning end to end and fix what that found\n\nAdds integration tests that drive the real worker through the real provider\nimplementations against mock GitLab and Outline servers, so the path from a resource\nconfig to an external call and back into the instance row is covered rather than only the\nrequest shapes. Three scenarios: a GitLab project provisioned into its team subgroup with\nthe extra-config template resolved per team, an Outline collection created private and\nbound to its group under the instance''s stable key, and a rejected member leaving the\ninstance partial with its external ID before a retry heals it. Each was confirmed to fail\nwhen the behaviour it covers is reverted.\n\nRunning the Keycloak provider against a real local Keycloak 26.6.3 turned up three things.\n\nThe documented role set does not exist. `manage-groups` is not a role in\n`realm-management`; group writes sit under `manage-users`. A lecturer following the docs\nwould have granted a role Keycloak does not have and hit 403 on every instance. Verified\nminimal working set: `manage-users` plus `view-users`.\n\nValidation passed for a service account that could not create groups. Neither the realm\nread nor a group read proves the write permission, so \"Validate\" reported success and\nprovisioning then failed 403 on every instance. Validation now also checks the granted\n`realm-management` roles, which are carried in the token itself and cost no extra request.\nA Keycloak that does not put client roles in the token is still accepted, since an absent\nclaim is not evidence of a missing role.\n\nConfirmed working against the live realm: idempotent group creation across repeated runs,\nboth known members added exactly once, an unknown email reported as a warning with the\ngroup retained, and the reported admin-console URL.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Key every Outline group on its permission\n\nThe single-permission case keyed its group on the bare stable key and only added the\npermission suffix once a second permission appeared. A team that gained a tutor after its\nfirst run would therefore look for a key that had never been used, create a second group,\nand leave the original orphaned but still bound to the collection.\n\nThe key now always carries the permission. The display name still drops the suffix when\nthere is only one group, so the common case reads the same in Outline.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Address review findings on the new code\n\nMigrations had no down files, unlike every other service, so the phase could not be rolled\nback. Added `0001`-`0003` down migrations and verified the full round trip against\nPostgreSQL 15.19: up, down, up again, down to empty. Writing them found a bug in the\n`0002` down path: rebuilding the status enum fails while the partial unique indexes still\ncarry a predicate bound to the old type, so they are now dropped and recreated around the\nswap. A row left in `partial` becomes `failed`, the retryable terminal state it would\notherwise have had.\n\nUpstream text still reached the persisted error message. `provider.HTTPError` keeps\nresponse bodies out of the string the UI renders, but three call sites parsed a per-item\nreason out of the body and put it straight into a warning. Those reasons are worth showing\na lecturer, so they now go through `provider.UpstreamReason`, which strips control\ncharacters, collapses the text onto one line and truncates it.\n\nError classification in two handlers:\n\n- `getResourceConfig` reported every failure as 404, so a database outage looked like a\n  missing row and nothing was logged. It now distinguishes `pgx.ErrNoRows` from a real\n  failure and logs the latter.\n- `validateProviderConfig` still answers 400 either way, since from the lecturer''s side an\n  unreachable provider and a bad token both mean \"these credentials do not work yet\", but\n  it now logs, which is what tells the two apart.\n\nValidation failures were logged at error level. They are ordinary bad requests answered\nwith 400, so they are warnings and no longer raise error-level alerts.\n\nFrontend: the Resource Configurations page guarded only one of its two queries, so while\nthe providers query was still loading the page claimed no providers were configured and\ndisabled the create button. Both are now required before the page renders, and retry\nrefetches both. The resource-type preselect no longer overwrites the stored value when\nediting an existing config.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Cover the project resource type in the lecturer journey\n\nThe e2e journey only exercised `group`, so nothing checked that the API accepts a GitLab\n`project` config or that a templated extra-config value is validated the way a name\ntemplate is. Adds both: an unresolvable placeholder in `parent_group_template` is refused\nat save time, and a well-formed project config is accepted.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop the remote building its own Tailwind sheet\n\nFixes the two genuinely failing pipeline jobs. The other three server jobs were cancelled\nby matrix fail-fast, not failing on their own.\n\nsingle-tailwind-sheet: the component still carried `tailwind.config.js` and a\n`src/styles.css` importing `tailwindcss`, from before main made core build the only\nstylesheet. A second sheet injected by a remote lands after core''s and overrides its\nutilities at equal specificity. Both files are gone and `src/index.js` now just imports\nthe bootstrap, matching every other component. Nothing is lost: core''s config already\nglobs `../*_component/src/**`, and its bundle contains\n`.border-amber-300{border-color:var(--color-amber-300)}` for a class only this component\nuses, so the utilities are compiled into core''s sheet.\n\nquality-server (infrastructure_setup): two staticcheck QF1012 findings in the Outline\ntests, now `fmt.Fprintf`.\n\n`make lint` did not run what CI runs, which is why both slipped through a green local run.\nIt now also runs `golangci-lint` on every service when installed, and the remote-styles\ncheck, so the pipeline is reproducible locally. All nine services report 0 issues.\n\nProjects are also created with an **empty repository** now, `initialize_with_readme`\ndefaulting to false instead of true. The phase provisions the group and the project; teams\npush their own repository. An initial commit made here gives the project a default branch\nand a history the team''s first push has to reconcile with. Courses that want a starting\ncommit can still opt in through extra config.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the review findings on the phase\n\nProvisioning:\n- GitLab addressed a project inside a team subgroup by the subgroup slug alone. The\n  namespaced path needs the parent group, so the lookup 404''d every time: the first run\n  created the project and every later one failed on a taken path. The group''s full path\n  is now carried through from the search or the create response.\n- The worker claimed every pending instance before resolving configs and targets. A\n  transient core failure left them in_progress, which is neither triggerable again (409)\n  nor retryable, so the phase was stuck. A failure now marks the claimed instances\n  failed, carrying the reason.\n- Startup recovery reset every in_progress row in the database, including another\n  replica''s live work, and reset them to pending, which nothing picks up. A sweeper now\n  marks only claims older than the worker''s own timeout as failed, so they are visible\n  and retryable.\n- Rancher granted its roleTemplateId to roles the permission mapping did not cover, even\n  when the lecturer never set one. An unmapped role is now a warning like everywhere\n  else, and a binding that already exists no longer keeps a retried instance partial.\n- An upstream reason was truncated by byte index, which can split a UTF-8 rune into a\n  string Postgres refuses to store.\n\nConfiguration:\n- Name templates were validated against empty template data, so a per_team config could\n  reference a student placeholder: at run time every team resolved to the same name and\n  shared one external resource. Placeholders now carry which scope fills them, from one\n  table that also drives resolution and the supported list.\n- A config can no longer be saved without the extra config its provider needs for the\n  resource type (a GitLab project without parent_group_template failed per instance).\n- A duplicate config answers 400 instead of a 500 out of the unique constraint.\n- Deleting a config cascades to its instances, PROMPT''s only record of external\n  resources it never deletes. That now takes an explicit confirmation.\n- The semester tag was validated trimmed but stored raw, so a template resolved to\n  \"-ios26--team-1\"; an invalid tag answered 500 instead of 400.\n- Execution refused to run when the phase had no config row, which nothing creates and\n  whose only field is optional. A missing row is now an empty config.\n\nStructure:\n- The copy package used a package-level singleton, an InitCopyModule that also\n  registered routes, and zero-value handler structs. It is now NewService +\n  RegisterRoutes, with the config endpoint in its own package like example_server.\n- HandlePhaseCopy wrote its own HTTP response on top of the one the SDK writes, so the\n  body held two JSON objects and core could not parse it. It only returns the error now,\n  and the test drives the registered route. The config status no longer reports a\n  database failure as \"nothing configured\".\n\nClient:\n- The remote hand-rolled an axios instance with its own JWT interceptor and a baseURL\n  frozen at module load. It now uses the shared createAuthenticatedAxiosInstance.\n- Server validation messages reached the toasts as \"Request failed with status code\n  400\". One describeError helper now reads the {\"error\": ...} body everywhere.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Address the remaining review notes\n\n- The .env templates shipped ENCRYPTION_KEY empty while the service refuses to start\n  without it, so `make servers` crash-looped on a fresh checkout. They now carry a\n  labelled placeholder key, the way they already carry the local database passwords.\n- The Setup page seeded its input from the query on every response, so a refetch (a\n  window refocus, or the invalidate after saving) overwrote whatever the lecturer was\n  typing. Local state is now an override that is handed back after a save.\n- GitLab re-attaches a group or project to the ID the instance recorded, so retrying an\n  instance whose name template changed no longer abandons the resource it points at with\n  the members already in it. A resource that is gone upstream still falls back to\n  resolving by path. The interface contract and the known limitations now say which\n  providers can do this.\n- The instance list had its API response aliased to the sqlc row, so a migration would\n  have changed the wire format silently. It is a DTO with a mapper now; the generated\n  spec is unchanged, which confirms the payload is the same.\n- Dropped the ./provide expose: its index was empty and nothing imports it, since the\n  phase has no student detail component (like certificate and presentation).\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Follow up on the merge and the claim release\n\n- readme: step 3 pointed at `make deps`, which the merge dropped in favour of main''s\n  per-service `make db`. The ports table names the example and presentation servers.\n- The writes that hand claimed instances back get their own context: the failure being\n  handled can be the run''s context expiring, and marking with a dead one would leave\n  exactly the claims this is meant to release.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Refresh the test inventory in the phase guide\n\n* Docs: Keep main''s docs lockfile after the merge\n\nThe branch''s older lockfile won a hunk renovate had bumped on main, silently\ndowngrading qs. Nothing about this phase needs a docs dependency change.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Copy nginx''s spa.conf into the remote''s image\n\nmain moved the remotes'' SPA fallback into a shared /etc/nginx/spa.conf that every\nclient Dockerfile copies out of the base image, and the bundled nginx.conf includes it.\nThe new component''s Dockerfile was written before that and did not copy it, so its\ncontainer died at startup (\"open() /etc/nginx/spa.conf failed\"), which in turn kept\nclient-core''s nginx from resolving the remote''s upstream and took the whole e2e stack\ndown. Its tsconfig also still listed the tailwind config that this branch removed.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Answer the privacy and phase deletion requests\n\nEvery other phase service on main registers the SDK privacy endpoints, and core does not\nconsult a capability flag before calling them: it fans a request out to every phase type\nof the courses the subject is in, by base URL. A missing route is a 404, one failed\nsubrequest marks the whole request failed, and the student is mailed that their deletion\ndid not go through. Since the demo seed puts this phase on the full course, that applied\nto the existing privacy suite as much as to a real deployment.\n\n- privacy/: the export carries the subject''s own resource instances with their provider\n  and resource type joined in; the deletion removes them and leaves team-scoped\n  instances, which belong to the team, alone.\n- coursePhaseDeletion/: implements the handler prompt-sdk v1.3.1 added (main has it in\n  team_allocation so far). It matters more here than elsewhere, because the rows for a\n  deleted phase include the encrypted credentials of external systems. Deleted from the\n  leaves up so a weakened cascade cannot leave orphans, inside a bounded transaction.\n- Both are declared in /info, and the SDK is now on v1.3.1 like every other service.\n- /info''s health probe no longer pings the database without a deadline.\n\nNeither deletion touches the external resources: this phase never deletes them, so a\ngroup named after a student outlives their deletion request. That is now stated in the\nphase guide next to the rest of the delete semantics.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Align the new phase with main''s dependency bumps\n\nThe merge of main brought the toolchain and shared dependencies forward for\nevery existing service and remote, but the infrastructure setup phase was\nadded after that bump landed, so it stayed on the older Go toolchain,\nprompt-sdk, sentry-go, logrus and rspack versions.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Record an instance''s outcome on a context of its own\n\nOnly failClaimed detached its writes from the run''s context. The per-instance\nwrites did not, so when the run''s context expired the failure could not be\nrecorded either: createWithRetry returned the context error and the write that\nwas meant to carry it failed on the same dead context.\n\nEvery remaining instance then stayed in_progress, where a trigger answers 409\nand Retry refuses to touch it, leaving the phase unusable until the stale-claim\nsweeper ran 45 minutes later. All three terminal writes now go through a context\ndetached from the run.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop an unresponsive provider from consuming a run\n\nEvery provider built its HTTP client with no timeout, so a host that accepts the\nconnection and never answers (a firewall dropping the response, a hung TLS\nhandshake) held the request open until the run''s 30 minute context expired. With\nfive worker goroutines that turns one unreachable provider into a run that\nprovisions nothing and never reaches its own retry logic.\n\nProviders now share a client with a per-request timeout, and each CreateResource\nattempt gets a deadline of its own, because a resource with many members issues\none request per member and a per-request timeout alone does not bound the call.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Converge a re-trigger on the instances already there\n\nBoth instance uniqueness indexes excluded failed rows, so a failed instance was\ninvisible to them and a second trigger inserted another row for the same target\ninstead of retrying the one that was there. The phase then showed two rows for\none team with no way to tell them apart, and resetting either of them to pending\ncollided with the other: a raw 23505 left the retry endpoint answering 500 with\na Postgres constraint message.\n\nA phase whose instances went partial had the opposite problem. Those rows are in\nthe index but are not counted as non-terminal, so the trigger passed its guard,\ninserted nothing, queued nothing, and still answered 202 ''execution started''. A\nlecturer retrying a cohort that had not signed in yet got a success toast over a\nno-op, which is the first thing a real run runs into.\n\nA (config, target) pair now carries exactly one instance, enforced by the\ndatabase, and a trigger converges on it: create what is missing, requeue what\nfailed or came back partial, leave what was created alone. The endpoint returns\nwhat it did so a trigger with nothing to do says so.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Keep the permission mapping editable after a run\n\nEditing a config was refused outright once it had any instance that was not\nfailed. That covers partial, and an unmapped role is exactly what leaves a run\npartial: every provider turns a member whose role has no permission into a\nwarning. Since a retry re-reads the stored mapping, fixing a typo in it meant\ndeleting every instance of the config first and provisioning the whole\nconfiguration again.\n\nWhat the resource is called and where it lives still cannot change, because the\ninstance would keep pointing at the object that exists. The mapping is separated\nfrom that identity: it is editable as long as no instance of the config is\nqueued or running. The dialog names the locked fields and the card is badged so\nthe rule is visible before the lecturer types anything.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Tell a Rancher duplicate apart from a rejected binding\n\nRancher answers 422 for a duplicate binding and for a validation error alike, so\ntreating every 422 as ''already bound'' reported real rejections as success. An\nunknown roleTemplateId (a typo in the extra config is enough) left the project\ncreated, the instance marked created, and the student never granted access, with\nnothing in the UI to reveal it.\n\nUpstream failures now carry their status and the body''s error code as a typed\nerror instead of a message the caller greps for. Rancher accepts 409, or a 422\ncarrying NotUnique, as a duplicate and reports every other 422 as a member\nwarning. GitLab''s conflict check reads the same status instead of matching on\n''HTTP 409''.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop an unsaved semester tag from naming resources\n\nThe setup page filled the semester tag field from the parent course, so it read\nback a tag the phase had never saved. The server resolves {{semesterTag}} from\nthe phase row alone, so a lecturer who saw ''ios26'' in a saved-looking field got\na GitLab group called ''-ios-team-1'' (or ''ios-team-1'' where the provider trims\nthe separator), and nothing said why.\n\nThe course tag is now the field''s placeholder and an explicit ''use the course\ntag'' action, and the page says that provisioning is refused while nothing is\nsaved. The trigger enforces that: a name template or templated extra-config\nvalue that uses the placeholder is a 400 until the tag exists.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Say what each execution row is about\n\nA row showed its own UUID and eight characters of a team or participation id.\nFor an iPraktikum-sized course that is 90 rows of hex with no way to tell which\nteam failed, what was created for it, or which config it came from, and a failed\nrow has no external URL either.\n\nInstances now carry the two labels the list needs: the team or student name as\ncore resolved it, and the name the provider was last asked to create, both\nwritten by the worker so even a row that failed upstream says what it was. The\nlist joins its config in for the provider and resource kind, and the page adds\nstatus counts that double as filters plus a search over the names.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Report team members that cannot be resolved\n\nTeam member emails are resolved from this phase''s participations, but a tutor is\nassigned to a team at course level. A tutor who does not take part in the phase\nmissed the lookup and was dropped from the member list with no trace, so a\ntutor permission mapping quietly granted nobody anything and the instance still\nreported created. The same held for any member whose participation carried no\nemail address.\n\nTarget resolution now explains who it could not turn into a member, and the\nworker carries those warnings onto the instance: the resource is created, the\ninstance comes back partial, and the message names the people who were left out.\nResolving a tutor who is not a phase participant would need a course-level\nparticipation lookup that core does not expose, so the docs say to add them to\nthe phase instead.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Refuse the committed placeholder encryption key\n\nThe .env templates ship a key that decodes to exactly 32 bytes so a fresh\ncheckout starts, and the startup gate accepted it. A deployment created with\n''cp .env.template .env'' therefore came up clean while encrypting GitLab personal\naccess tokens, Slack bot tokens and Rancher secret keys with a key anyone can\nread out of the repository.\n\nThat value is now refused unless DEBUG=true, which is what local development and\nthe e2e stack run with, so only a real deployment is affected and it fails at\nstartup rather than silently.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Explain a Slack name conflict it cannot see into\n\nconversations.list returns a private channel only to an app that belongs to it,\nso a private channel a human created holds the name and never shows up. The\nlookup after a name_taken then failed with ''not found after name conflict'',\nwhich reads like a bug in PROMPT rather than the one thing a lecturer can act\non. No bot-token call reveals such a channel, so this is the platform''s limit,\nnot something the phase can work around.\n\nThe lookup now also lists public channels, not to adopt one (that would widen\nwho can read a team''s material) but so a name taken by a public channel is\nreported as exactly that. Both cases name the channel and say what to do, and\nthe limitation is documented next to the idempotency claim.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Only retry a provider failure that could succeed\n\nEvery provider error was retried three times with backoff, including the ones\nthat describe the request rather than the moment: a revoked token, an invalid\nname, a parent group id that does not exist. Across 90 instances at five workers\nthat turned one configuration mistake into minutes of provider load before the\nphase reported anything.\n\nAn upstream 4xx now fails the instance on the first attempt, with 408 and 429 as\nthe exceptions, while an error carrying no HTTP status (a dial failure, a\ntimeout, a parse error) is retried as before.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop the upsert dialogs from wiping what is typed\n\nBoth upsert dialogs seeded their form from an effect that depended on the array\nof available provider types. The parent rebuilds that array inline on every\nrender, so any re-render while the dialog was open (a window-focus refetch of the\nprovider list is enough) re-ran the effect and cleared the form, discarding a\nhalf-typed access token or an edited name template.\n\nThe form is seeded when the dialog opens, which is the only moment it should be.\n\nDeleting a resource config also invalidates the instances query now: the delete\ncascades to its instances in SQL, and the execution list went on rendering them\nwith live retry and delete buttons. The provider card already did this.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Close the smaller gaps from the review\n\nDeleting a provider config answered 400 for every failure, including a database\nerror, because the handler could not tell a rejected request from a broken one.\nUnknown provider types are now marked as validation failures and everything else\nanswers 500.\n\nThe permission mapping accepted any role string, but resolution only ever\nassigns ''student'' and ''tutor'', so a typo produced a row that reached nobody. The\ndialog offers those two (keeping whatever a saved config already names) and says\nthat keycloak and slack ignore the mapping entirely, which nothing had mentioned.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Maximilian Rapp <maximilian.rapp@tum.de>\nCo-authored-by: Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Josef Schmid <51161233+JGStyle@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"4c52003646a98f22a1c859801f4aea1076af33b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/4c52003646a98f22a1c859801f4aea1076af33b1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002","verification":{"payload":"tree 4c52003646a98f22a1c859801f4aea1076af33b1\nparent 0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961\nauthor Matthias Linhuber <github@linhuber.org> 1789049211 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789049211 +0100\n\nNew Phase: Infrastructure Setup Phase (#1593)\n\n* Init\n\n* Add setup config and auth-aware infrastructure provisioning\n\n* Fix infrastructure setup CI issues\n\n* Add infrastructure setup components and update environment configurations\n\n* feat: add infrastructure setup interfaces and network mutations for resource and provider configurations\n\n- Introduced interfaces for provider configuration, resource configuration, resource instance, and setup configuration.\n- Implemented network mutations for creating, updating, deleting, and validating resource and provider configurations.\n- Added queries to fetch instances, provider configurations, and resource configurations.\n- Enhanced ExecutionPage and ProvidersPage to utilize new network queries and mutations.\n- Updated ResourceConfigPage and SetupConfigPage to manage resource and setup configurations with improved UI components.\n\n* Refactor resource instance management and enhance provider configuration handling\n\n- Added new SQL queries for counting non-terminal instances, creating, deleting, and retrieving resource instances.\n- Updated the CreateResourceInstance and DeleteResourceInstance functions to use structured parameters.\n- Refactored the service layer to handle provider configuration deletion and validation more effectively.\n- Enhanced the resource configuration service to support deletion and retrieval using structured parameters.\n- Updated SQLC configuration to emit JSON tags in camel case and support nullable UUIDs.\n- Improved error handling in service methods to ensure robustness during instance creation and retries.\n\n* feat: enhance infrastructure setup with new API endpoints and documentation\n\n* feat: add comprehensive tests for infrastructure setup, provider configurations, and resource management\n\n* feat: Refactor infrastructure setup API with DTOs and validation\n\n- Introduced DTOs for phase and provider configurations to streamline API responses and requests.\n- Implemented validation logic for upsert requests in both phase and provider configurations.\n- Updated API routes to utilize new DTO structures for improved clarity and maintainability.\n- Changed server host from localhost:8089 to localhost:8091 across documentation and code.\n- Enhanced error handling in API responses with retry options for loading configurations.\n- Added validation for resource configuration creation and updates, ensuring required fields and formats.\n- Refactored service methods to return DTOs instead of raw database models for better abstraction.\n\n* refactor: streamline error handling in ExecutionPage, ProvidersPage, and SetupConfigPage\n\n* refactor: simplify name template validation by removing template parsing\n\n* refactor: source teams via phase configurator instead of manual import\n\nMove the Infrastructure Setup phase''s team/student sourcing from a\nmanual sibling-phase picker to the standard PROMPT phase-IO mechanism,\nmirroring the Assessment phase. The phase configurator now wires the\nteams and teamAllocation inputs; core''s dependency graph drives\nresolution.\n\n- Declare `teams` (phase-level) and `teamAllocation` (participation-level)\n  required inputs in core''s initInfrastructureSetup.\n- Rewrite execution/target_resolver to call FetchAndMerge* with this\n  phase''s own coursePhaseID, with local parseTeams/parsePersons helpers\n  modeled on the Assessment phase.\n- Drop team_source_course_phase_id and student_source_course_phase_id\n  from course_phase_config (schema, queries, regenerated sqlc).\n- Strip the same fields from the upsert DTO, service, validation, and\n  tests.\n- Drop the sourcePhase flag from ConfigHandler; report semesterTag,\n  providerConfig, and resourceConfig readiness instead.\n- Register /info in main.go so the management console''s System Status\n  page reports the service as healthy.\n- Simplify SetupConfigPage to only manage the semester tag and point\n  lecturers at the phase configurator for input wiring.\n- Refresh Swagger docs to match the new DTO shapes and descriptions.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: address CodeQL allocation overflow + gitignore the new db volume\n\n- Drop the len(team.Members)+len(team.Tutors) sum from the make() capacity\n  hint that CodeQL flagged as potentially overflowing. Use len(team.Members)\n  alone; tutor appends grow the slice dynamically. The over-allocation is\n  negligible for the team sizes this code handles.\n- Add postgres_infrastructure_setup_data/ to .gitignore so the docker\n  volume directory does not appear in git status.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore: regenerate core sqlc output after main merge\n\nThe merge with main reordered Test*TypeExists functions in the\ngenerated sqlc Go code; CI''s \"Check sqlc generated files are current\"\nstep fails until the file is regenerated and committed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix biome lint and wire phase into CI matrices\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Simplify provider and page code without behavior change\n\n- Collapse rancher lookupUserByEmail into a single struct and index check\n- Extract keycloak group lookup into a shared searchGroupByName helper\n- Drop dead no-auth RetryFailedInstance wrapper and unused CopyService conn field\n- Reuse shared LoadingPage/ErrorPage on the resource config page\n- Inline the StatusBadge label and remove the unused getResourceConfig query\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Regenerate API spec after DTO package rename\n\n* Self Team Allocation: Revert unrelated timeframe timezone change\n\nThe timeframe column is TIMESTAMP without zone, so pgtype.Timestamp.Time is\nalready UTC-located and the original instant comparison was correct.\nRebuilding both bounds in time.Local shifted the survey window by the host\noffset when running the server outside Docker.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix environment, routing and startup blockers\n\n- .env.template: drop the alias block that redefined DB_HOST_/DB_PORT_ for team\n  allocation, self team allocation, assessment, interview and certificate from\n  variables that do not exist. Those assignments came after the real definitions\n  and emptied the database configuration of five running services.\n- Register the SDK phase-config endpoint on the phase-scoped group. It is mounted\n  as a bare /config, so on the global group it resolved without :coursePhaseID and\n  returned 500 on every call while /info advertised the capability.\n- Restrict the phase routes to admins and lecturers. These carry external provider\n  credentials, and the client already gated its pages the same way, so an editor\n  could only reach them by calling the API directly.\n- Wait for Keycloak before starting: the server initialises the auth middleware\n  through synchronous OIDC discovery and exits when it is unavailable.\n- Read INFRASTRUCTURE_SETUP_HOST from window.env. It is not part of the shared\n  state EnvType, so the normalised env object dropped it and the base URL fell\n  back to the page origin, which has no proxy in local development.\n- Add INFRASTRUCTURE_SETUP_HOST and the Sentry DSN to the templates and compose\n  files, and the local database overrides to .env.dev.template.\n- Validate ENCRYPTION_KEY at startup instead of on the first credential write.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Make execution safe to run twice and report partial results\n\nProvisioning could run twice over the same instance and silently create\nresources nobody was added to.\n\n- Claim pending instances in a single UPDATE ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED instead\n  of listing them and marking them in_progress afterwards, so two overlapping\n  runs cannot pick up the same row.\n- Guard the trigger with a transaction-scoped advisory lock around the\n  non-terminal count and the inserts. Counting first and inserting after left a\n  window where two callers both saw an idle phase. The second caller now gets 409.\n  Targets are resolved before the transaction because that calls core over HTTP.\n- Refuse to trigger a phase whose providers have no credentials, which is the\n  state a copied phase starts in. Previously every instance was created and then\n  failed one by one on decryption.\n- Replace the catch-all status update with MarkInstanceCreated, MarkInstancePartial\n  and MarkInstanceFailed. The old query wrote all four columns unconditionally, so\n  a failure blanked an external ID recorded by an earlier success.\n- Report members that could not be granted access as warnings on the resource and\n  record the instance as partial. GitLab and Rancher used to fail the whole\n  instance on the first bad member; Keycloak, Slack and Outline skipped them and\n  reported success, leaving an empty group with nothing to show the lecturer.\n  Partial instances can be retried and heal once the users exist upstream.\n- Retry now returns the row it reset, so retrying an unknown instance is a 404 and\n  retrying one that is not failed or partial is a 409. It previously reported 202\n  and started a worker in both cases.\n- Resolve targets once per scope per run rather than once per instance, which\n  issued two core requests for every resource.\n- Drop DeleteResource from the provider interface. It had no caller, and since\n  providers adopt resources by name it could have removed something the course\n  does not own. Instance deletion stays a PROMPT-only operation, as the dialog\n  already stated.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Make a copied phase report and behave as unconfigured\n\nA copied phase kept its provider rows but not the credentials, and still reported\nitself as fully configured. The lecturer saw a ready phase, triggered execution,\nand every instance failed on decryption.\n\n- Copy the course_phase_config row as well. HandlePhaseConfig returns all-false\n  as soon as that row is missing, so the semester tag and everything after it were\n  reported as unconfigured no matter what else had been copied.\n- Run the whole copy in one transaction so a failure part-way cannot leave a\n  half-copied phase, and skip resource configs that already exist on the target so\n  repeating a copy does not duplicate them.\n- Report only providers holding credentials as configured, expose that as a\n  ''configured'' flag on the API, and show ''credentials required'' on the provider card.\n- Refuse to create or update a resource config whose provider has no credentials,\n  and leave such providers out of the resource config picker.\n- Validate the resource type against the provider that has to create it. It was\n  free text passed to providers that never read it, so the field implied a choice\n  that had no effect; the dialog now offers only the supported kinds.\n- Answer 400 rather than 500 when a resource config request is rejected.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the provider HTTP integrations\n\nEach provider had a defect that only shows against a real API.\n\nGitLab\n- Search the configured parent''s subgroups and compare the path exactly. Matching\n  a full_path suffix across every visible group also matched another course''s\n  group of the same name, adding the students to it.\n- Drop min_access_level=50 from the group search. A maintainer token never saw the\n  groups it had created, recreated them, and failed on the taken path.\n- Invite members through /invitations instead of resolving a user ID first.\n  /users?search= only returns the email field to admin tokens, so member adds\n  failed after the group had already been created. Invitations also work for\n  students who have not signed in yet.\n\nRancher\n- Resolve users through the principals search endpoint and confirm the hit against\n  the requested address. The v3 user schema has no email field, so ?email= was not\n  a filter at all and Rancher returned an unfiltered list whose first entry, the\n  local admin, was bound into the student project.\n- Use the principal ID Rancher returns rather than assembling local://<id>, which\n  is wrong on any LDAP- or OIDC-backed instance.\n\nSlack\n- Form-encode requests. conversations.list and users.lookupByEmail are documented\n  as form-only and reject a JSON body.\n- Declare the pagination response inside the loop. A page without response_metadata\n  left the previous cursor in place, so the same page was requested until the\n  30-minute context expired.\n- Stop excluding archived channels when resolving a name conflict. The archived\n  channel owns the name, so skipping it turned name_taken into a permanent\n  ''channel not found''.\n\nOutline\n- Check ok on the membership call, which was ignored, and use collections.add_user.\n- Paginate collections.list; a collection past the first page was never found and\n  a duplicate was created under the same name.\n\nAll four are covered by httptest servers. Slack gained an overridable base URL so\nits API calls can be exercised at all.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Reject name templates and credentials that cannot work\n\n- ResolveName now fails on a placeholder it does not know instead of leaving the\n  raw {{...}} in the name of a real external resource, and resource config\n  validation runs the same check so a typo is caught when the template is saved\n  rather than when provisioning runs.\n- Remove {{.CourseName}}, {{.TeamIndex}} and {{.Year}}. They were documented but\n  never populated by either resolver, so they always rendered as an empty string\n  or 0. The semester tag keeps all three of its spellings, since saved templates\n  may use any of them.\n- Require credential values to be strings and reject unknown fields. A nested\n  object was previously encrypted without complaint and only surfaced much later,\n  as a 500 when the worker tried to build the provider.\n- Answer 400 rather than 500 when provider credentials are rejected.\n- Seed the semester tag field only when the config loads. Refilling it whenever it\n  was empty meant an intentionally cleared tag could never be saved.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Cover the execution worker and target resolver with tests\n\nThe worker and the target resolver had no tests at all, though they hold the retry,\nconcurrency and status logic.\n\n- worker: an instance reaches created, a member warning yields partial with the\n  external ID intact, retries stop at the limit and mark the instance failed, a\n  transient failure still succeeds, a vanished target and an unresolvable name\n  template fail without calling the provider, and a crash-interrupted instance is\n  recovered at startup.\n- Two workers racing on the same phase call the provider exactly once, which is the\n  regression the atomic claim exists to prevent.\n- target resolver: parseTeams and parsePersons skip malformed upstream entries and\n  only reject a payload that is not a list; targetIndex resolves both scopes.\n- provider config: non-string and unknown credential fields are rejected, and every\n  provider reports its supported resource types.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Align tooling with the other phases and rewrite the guide\n\n- Move the component to rspack 2.1 and yarn 4.18, matching core and every other\n  component. It was a major version behind, so the Module Federation runtime in the\n  remote did not match the host, and the lockfile carried two rspack trees with all\n  their platform bindings. Also rename the cache type to the 2.x spelling and use\n  node: import specifiers, leaving the config identical to the certificate phase''s\n  apart from the component name and port.\n- Bump prompt-sdk to v1.2.3, the version the other phase services use.\n- Register the service with the swagger pre-commit hook. CI already checked the spec\n  was current, so an annotation change would have failed there instead.\n- Add the phase to the AGENTS.md inventory, the glossary and the stress-test service\n  list, and pin its database to the same postgres patch as the rest.\n- Rewrite docs/contributor/infrastructure-setup-phase.md. It documented a port, a\n  file layout, an API surface and a test suite that never existed. It now describes\n  the real routes and roles, the partial status, the concurrency guarantees, why\n  external resources are never deleted, and what copying a phase does.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Add end-to-end coverage\n\n- Wire the phase into the e2e stack: its own server, remote and database in\n  docker-compose.e2e.yml, the /infrastructure-setup proxy locations that Traefik\n  provides in prod, the host and database variables, a fixed test encryption key,\n  and a readiness poll in global-setup.\n- Seed a standalone phase on the full course with two participants, so a\n  per_student resource config resolves to real provisioning targets.\n- mf-smoke.spec.ts proves the remote loads and renders inside the core shell,\n  which is the failure mode the window.env host fix addresses.\n- lecturer-journey.spec.ts drives the phase through its own API: an unconfigured\n  phase reports nothing ready, a resource config is refused before its provider\n  has credentials, credentials never come back over the wire, unknown placeholders\n  and unsupported resource types are rejected, and a second trigger while work is\n  in flight returns 409 without creating a second set of instances. The provider\n  points at a blackholed TEST-NET address so the instances stay non-terminal and\n  that conflict is deterministic rather than a race against the worker.\n- Both specs reset the phase before and after they run, so a rerun against a live\n  stack starts from the same state.\n- Return [] rather than null from the instances endpoint when nothing is\n  provisioned, which the empty-phase assertions surfaced.\n\n* Self Team Allocation: Restore the timeframe timezone handling\n\nReverts 1303907. Removing that hunk was wrong: TestAssignTeamRoute,\nTestAssignTeamRouteTeamFull and TestLeaveTeamRoute fail without it on any host\nthat is not on UTC.\n\nThe timeframe column is TIMESTAMP without a zone and the value is written from a\nlocal time.Time, so the stored digits are local wall-clock and reading them back\nyields a UTC-located time carrying those digits. Comparing that against\ntime.Now() is therefore off by the host offset, which is why the tests pass in\nCI and fail on a UTC+2 machine. Rebuilding both bounds in time.Local restores\nthe intended instant.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Adopt SDK startup helpers in the phase service\n\nReplace the service-local initKeycloak and runMigrations with\npromptSDK.InitPhaseKeycloak and sdkUtils.RunMigrations, matching the\nconvention the other phase services now follow, and bump prompt-sdk to\nv1.3.0 where those helpers live.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Copy the phase component manifest in the clients base image\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the provider defects that break provisioning\n\nGitLab:\n\n- parent_group_id could not be used at all. It was declared as *int, but credential\n  values are validated and stored as strings, so decoding a configured value failed\n  with \"cannot unmarshal string into Go struct field parent_group_id of type int\" and\n  every GitLab operation errored out. It is now a string parsed on use, and a\n  non-numeric value is rejected when the credentials are validated rather than on the\n  first provisioning run.\n- The group search is paginated and re-resolves after a failed create. Search is a\n  substring match, so \"ios-team-1\" also matches \"ios-team-10\"; with enough sibling\n  groups the exact match fell outside the first page, the provider concluded \"absent\",\n  and the create then failed on a taken path.\n- An unmapped role or an unrecognized permission string silently became Guest. A\n  GitLab Guest cannot read code in a private project, so the team was reported as\n  provisioned while having no repository access. Both are warnings now, which makes\n  the instance partial and retryable.\n\nKeycloak:\n\n- A create losing a race answered 409 and failed the instance through all three\n  retries. The conflict now re-resolves the group.\n- The group search asks for exact=true and pages, for the same substring-match reason\n  as GitLab.\n- The reported URL pointed at the admin REST endpoint, which answers 401 JSON when the\n  link is opened. It now points at the admin console.\n- Added the mocked-HTTP coverage the provider had none of: create, adoption, exact\n  search, conflict recovery, and the unknown-member warning path.\n\nOutline:\n\n- users.list never checked ok. A workspace-wide permission or rate-limit failure\n  returns HTTP 200 with ok=false and no data, which was reported as \"outline user not\n  found for email\", blaming the student for a token problem.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Add the identity and safety plumbing the providers need\n\nGroundwork for per-team GitLab projects and the Outline collection binding. No provider\nbehaviour changes yet.\n\nStable identity. `CreateResourceInput` carries a `StableKey`\n(`prompt:<coursePhaseID>:<resourceConfigID>:<targetID>`) and the instance''s\n`ExistingExternalID`. A provider can now pair an external object with PROMPT and prove\nownership before adopting a resource, and a retried instance re-attaches by ID instead of\nlooking the resource up by name. Both are derived from data the instance already has, so\nthere is no migration.\n\nTemplated extra config. A provider declares which extra-config keys are name templates\nthrough `TemplatedExtraConfigKeys`. Only those are resolved, and they are validated with\nthe name-template rules on create and update, so an unresolvable placeholder is rejected\nat save time rather than reaching a real resource name mid-run. Every other extra-config\nvalue stays literal, so Rancher''s `roleTemplateId` is untouched and a setting can still\ncontain braces.\n\nConfig edit lock. Editing a config that already has a non-failed instance is refused.\nThe partial unique indexes on `resource_instance` mean a live instance blocks a second\none for the same target, so an edited config never re-provisions: the row would describe\none resource while a differently named one existed externally, invisible from the UI. A\npermission-mapping edit is refused for the same reason, since it cannot reach memberships\nthat were already granted.\n\nError sanitisation. Providers formatted the raw upstream response body into their errors,\nand the worker persists that string in `resource_instance.error_message`, which the UI\nrenders. Bodies can carry tokens, internal hostnames or other users'' data. All eleven\ncall sites now go through `provider.HTTPError`, which reports method, path and status\nonly, and logs a truncated body at debug level.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Provision GitLab projects, not just groups\n\nThe phase could only create GitLab groups, so a team got an empty subgroup and every\nrepository was made by hand. `CreateResource` also never read `input.ResourceType`: it\ncreated a group whatever was asked for, which meant a config row carrying a resource type\nthe API validation had not seen would create a group named after the project template and\nreport it as a success.\n\nGitLab now supports `group` and `project`, `CreateResource` dispatches on the resource\ntype, and an unrecognised type is refused rather than falling through to group creation.\n\nA project resource creates the team''s subgroup and the project inside it. The subgroup is\nan explicit side effect rather than a tracked dependency: instances carry no ordering, so\na project cannot wait for a separately configured group row. Both paths adopt by exact\npath under the same parent, so a project config and a group config for the same team\nconverge on one subgroup instead of racing.\n\nA project requires both the provider''s `parent_group_id` and a `parent_group_template`\nresolving to a team subgroup. Without them the project would land in the token owner''s\npersonal namespace, or need a top-level group most instances forbid, so both are refused\nrather than guessed.\n\nMembers are granted access on the subgroup only. GitLab group members inherit access to\nevery project in the group, so per-project invitations would add nothing, and a test\nasserts none are sent.\n\nExistence is decided by an exact lookup on the URL-encoded namespaced path, not by\nparsing the create error. GitLab''s duplicate-path response is the least specified part of\nthat surface: create answers 400 with a field-keyed message map while the fork endpoint\nanswers 409 with a flat array of sentences. The create error is only a signal to re-run\nthe exact lookup, which also covers losing a race.\n\nVisibility is sent explicitly and defaults to private. Omitting it falls back to the\ninstance-wide `default_project_visibility`, which an administrator may have set to\npublic, so a team repository could have been created world-readable.\n`initialize_with_readme` defaults to true so the repository has a branch to clone.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Bind an Outline group to each team''s collection\n\nA collection was created with `permission: \"read\"`, which in Outline means every member\nof the workspace can read it: `Collection.isPrivate` is false whenever a permission is\nset, and the read policy then returns true without checking any membership. Every team''s\ncollection was readable by every student in the workspace, and the per-user memberships\nadded afterwards granted nothing beyond that.\n\nCollections are now created private, by omitting `permission`, and access is granted\nthrough a group bound to the collection.\n\nWhat this does, and what it cannot do. Outline has no OIDC group synchronisation: its\nOIDC plugin reads no groups claim, so collection visibility can never be decided from a\ntoken. Keycloak authenticates the user; Outline authorises against its own group\nmembership. When both are configured, PROMPT puts the same snapshot of team members into\nthe Keycloak group and into the Outline group, and binds the Outline group to the\ncollection. Nothing is synchronised afterwards and no membership is ever removed, which\nis why teams must be settled before provisioning runs.\n\nThe group is keyed on Outline''s `externalId`, using the instance''s stable key rather than\na display name, so a renamed collection or an edited template still resolves to the same\ngroup.\n\nCollections are no longer adopted by name alone. A created collection carries an\nownership marker in its description, and a same-named collection without that marker is\nrefused with an actionable error. Outline collections share one flat workspace namespace,\nso name adoption could hand a team read access to an unrelated collection. A retried\ninstance re-attaches by its recorded ID, so the name decides nothing on the path that\nmatters.\n\nMembers whose roles map to different permissions get one group each. A group carries a\nsingle collection permission, so picking one for a team of students plus a tutor would\nsilently over- or under-grant. Split groups get a distinguishable display name.\n`collections.add_group` always sends the permission explicitly, because Outline defaults\nthat call to read_write.\n\nOnce the collection exists every later step contributes warnings instead of failing, so\nthe instance stays partial and retryable with its external ID intact. Member lookup\nresolves all emails in one or two calls instead of paging the workspace once per member.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Document what the phase provisions and what it cannot\n\nFrontend:\n\n- The resource-type dropdown already rendered every kind the server reports, so GitLab''s\n  new `project` needed no change; only the \"providers support exactly one kind\" comment\n  was stale.\n- The extra-config field explains that most values are literal and lists the two keys\n  that are name templates, per provider and resource type.\n- The Resource Configurations page states how Outline access actually works whenever an\n  Outline config exists, and points out an Outline config that has no Keycloak group\n  config at the same scope. The limitation belongs where a lecturer is making the\n  decision, not only in the contributor docs.\n\nDocs:\n\n- GitLab now lists `group` and `project`, with the iPraktikum layout and the reason a\n  project requires both a parent group ID and a parent group template.\n- A new Outline access section says plainly that Outline has no OIDC group\n  synchronisation and cannot decide collection visibility from a token: Keycloak\n  authenticates, Outline authorises from its own group membership, and PROMPT provisions\n  the same member snapshot into both groups when both are configured.\n- Documented that memberships are never removed, so teams must be settled before\n  provisioning, and that a config is frozen once it has provisioned.\n- Added a Known limitations section collecting these, including that Slack, Outline and\n  Rancher are covered only by mocked tests against the published contracts.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Verify provisioning end to end and fix what that found\n\nAdds integration tests that drive the real worker through the real provider\nimplementations against mock GitLab and Outline servers, so the path from a resource\nconfig to an external call and back into the instance row is covered rather than only the\nrequest shapes. Three scenarios: a GitLab project provisioned into its team subgroup with\nthe extra-config template resolved per team, an Outline collection created private and\nbound to its group under the instance''s stable key, and a rejected member leaving the\ninstance partial with its external ID before a retry heals it. Each was confirmed to fail\nwhen the behaviour it covers is reverted.\n\nRunning the Keycloak provider against a real local Keycloak 26.6.3 turned up three things.\n\nThe documented role set does not exist. `manage-groups` is not a role in\n`realm-management`; group writes sit under `manage-users`. A lecturer following the docs\nwould have granted a role Keycloak does not have and hit 403 on every instance. Verified\nminimal working set: `manage-users` plus `view-users`.\n\nValidation passed for a service account that could not create groups. Neither the realm\nread nor a group read proves the write permission, so \"Validate\" reported success and\nprovisioning then failed 403 on every instance. Validation now also checks the granted\n`realm-management` roles, which are carried in the token itself and cost no extra request.\nA Keycloak that does not put client roles in the token is still accepted, since an absent\nclaim is not evidence of a missing role.\n\nConfirmed working against the live realm: idempotent group creation across repeated runs,\nboth known members added exactly once, an unknown email reported as a warning with the\ngroup retained, and the reported admin-console URL.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Key every Outline group on its permission\n\nThe single-permission case keyed its group on the bare stable key and only added the\npermission suffix once a second permission appeared. A team that gained a tutor after its\nfirst run would therefore look for a key that had never been used, create a second group,\nand leave the original orphaned but still bound to the collection.\n\nThe key now always carries the permission. The display name still drops the suffix when\nthere is only one group, so the common case reads the same in Outline.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Address review findings on the new code\n\nMigrations had no down files, unlike every other service, so the phase could not be rolled\nback. Added `0001`-`0003` down migrations and verified the full round trip against\nPostgreSQL 15.19: up, down, up again, down to empty. Writing them found a bug in the\n`0002` down path: rebuilding the status enum fails while the partial unique indexes still\ncarry a predicate bound to the old type, so they are now dropped and recreated around the\nswap. A row left in `partial` becomes `failed`, the retryable terminal state it would\notherwise have had.\n\nUpstream text still reached the persisted error message. `provider.HTTPError` keeps\nresponse bodies out of the string the UI renders, but three call sites parsed a per-item\nreason out of the body and put it straight into a warning. Those reasons are worth showing\na lecturer, so they now go through `provider.UpstreamReason`, which strips control\ncharacters, collapses the text onto one line and truncates it.\n\nError classification in two handlers:\n\n- `getResourceConfig` reported every failure as 404, so a database outage looked like a\n  missing row and nothing was logged. It now distinguishes `pgx.ErrNoRows` from a real\n  failure and logs the latter.\n- `validateProviderConfig` still answers 400 either way, since from the lecturer''s side an\n  unreachable provider and a bad token both mean \"these credentials do not work yet\", but\n  it now logs, which is what tells the two apart.\n\nValidation failures were logged at error level. They are ordinary bad requests answered\nwith 400, so they are warnings and no longer raise error-level alerts.\n\nFrontend: the Resource Configurations page guarded only one of its two queries, so while\nthe providers query was still loading the page claimed no providers were configured and\ndisabled the create button. Both are now required before the page renders, and retry\nrefetches both. The resource-type preselect no longer overwrites the stored value when\nediting an existing config.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Cover the project resource type in the lecturer journey\n\nThe e2e journey only exercised `group`, so nothing checked that the API accepts a GitLab\n`project` config or that a templated extra-config value is validated the way a name\ntemplate is. Adds both: an unresolvable placeholder in `parent_group_template` is refused\nat save time, and a well-formed project config is accepted.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop the remote building its own Tailwind sheet\n\nFixes the two genuinely failing pipeline jobs. The other three server jobs were cancelled\nby matrix fail-fast, not failing on their own.\n\nsingle-tailwind-sheet: the component still carried `tailwind.config.js` and a\n`src/styles.css` importing `tailwindcss`, from before main made core build the only\nstylesheet. A second sheet injected by a remote lands after core''s and overrides its\nutilities at equal specificity. Both files are gone and `src/index.js` now just imports\nthe bootstrap, matching every other component. Nothing is lost: core''s config already\nglobs `../*_component/src/**`, and its bundle contains\n`.border-amber-300{border-color:var(--color-amber-300)}` for a class only this component\nuses, so the utilities are compiled into core''s sheet.\n\nquality-server (infrastructure_setup): two staticcheck QF1012 findings in the Outline\ntests, now `fmt.Fprintf`.\n\n`make lint` did not run what CI runs, which is why both slipped through a green local run.\nIt now also runs `golangci-lint` on every service when installed, and the remote-styles\ncheck, so the pipeline is reproducible locally. All nine services report 0 issues.\n\nProjects are also created with an **empty repository** now, `initialize_with_readme`\ndefaulting to false instead of true. The phase provisions the group and the project; teams\npush their own repository. An initial commit made here gives the project a default branch\nand a history the team''s first push has to reconcile with. Courses that want a starting\ncommit can still opt in through extra config.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Fix the review findings on the phase\n\nProvisioning:\n- GitLab addressed a project inside a team subgroup by the subgroup slug alone. The\n  namespaced path needs the parent group, so the lookup 404''d every time: the first run\n  created the project and every later one failed on a taken path. The group''s full path\n  is now carried through from the search or the create response.\n- The worker claimed every pending instance before resolving configs and targets. A\n  transient core failure left them in_progress, which is neither triggerable again (409)\n  nor retryable, so the phase was stuck. A failure now marks the claimed instances\n  failed, carrying the reason.\n- Startup recovery reset every in_progress row in the database, including another\n  replica''s live work, and reset them to pending, which nothing picks up. A sweeper now\n  marks only claims older than the worker''s own timeout as failed, so they are visible\n  and retryable.\n- Rancher granted its roleTemplateId to roles the permission mapping did not cover, even\n  when the lecturer never set one. An unmapped role is now a warning like everywhere\n  else, and a binding that already exists no longer keeps a retried instance partial.\n- An upstream reason was truncated by byte index, which can split a UTF-8 rune into a\n  string Postgres refuses to store.\n\nConfiguration:\n- Name templates were validated against empty template data, so a per_team config could\n  reference a student placeholder: at run time every team resolved to the same name and\n  shared one external resource. Placeholders now carry which scope fills them, from one\n  table that also drives resolution and the supported list.\n- A config can no longer be saved without the extra config its provider needs for the\n  resource type (a GitLab project without parent_group_template failed per instance).\n- A duplicate config answers 400 instead of a 500 out of the unique constraint.\n- Deleting a config cascades to its instances, PROMPT''s only record of external\n  resources it never deletes. That now takes an explicit confirmation.\n- The semester tag was validated trimmed but stored raw, so a template resolved to\n  \"-ios26--team-1\"; an invalid tag answered 500 instead of 400.\n- Execution refused to run when the phase had no config row, which nothing creates and\n  whose only field is optional. A missing row is now an empty config.\n\nStructure:\n- The copy package used a package-level singleton, an InitCopyModule that also\n  registered routes, and zero-value handler structs. It is now NewService +\n  RegisterRoutes, with the config endpoint in its own package like example_server.\n- HandlePhaseCopy wrote its own HTTP response on top of the one the SDK writes, so the\n  body held two JSON objects and core could not parse it. It only returns the error now,\n  and the test drives the registered route. The config status no longer reports a\n  database failure as \"nothing configured\".\n\nClient:\n- The remote hand-rolled an axios instance with its own JWT interceptor and a baseURL\n  frozen at module load. It now uses the shared createAuthenticatedAxiosInstance.\n- Server validation messages reached the toasts as \"Request failed with status code\n  400\". One describeError helper now reads the {\"error\": ...} body everywhere.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Address the remaining review notes\n\n- The .env templates shipped ENCRYPTION_KEY empty while the service refuses to start\n  without it, so `make servers` crash-looped on a fresh checkout. They now carry a\n  labelled placeholder key, the way they already carry the local database passwords.\n- The Setup page seeded its input from the query on every response, so a refetch (a\n  window refocus, or the invalidate after saving) overwrote whatever the lecturer was\n  typing. Local state is now an override that is handed back after a save.\n- GitLab re-attaches a group or project to the ID the instance recorded, so retrying an\n  instance whose name template changed no longer abandons the resource it points at with\n  the members already in it. A resource that is gone upstream still falls back to\n  resolving by path. The interface contract and the known limitations now say which\n  providers can do this.\n- The instance list had its API response aliased to the sqlc row, so a migration would\n  have changed the wire format silently. It is a DTO with a mapper now; the generated\n  spec is unchanged, which confirms the payload is the same.\n- Dropped the ./provide expose: its index was empty and nothing imports it, since the\n  phase has no student detail component (like certificate and presentation).\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Follow up on the merge and the claim release\n\n- readme: step 3 pointed at `make deps`, which the merge dropped in favour of main''s\n  per-service `make db`. The ports table names the example and presentation servers.\n- The writes that hand claimed instances back get their own context: the failure being\n  handled can be the run''s context expiring, and marking with a dead one would leave\n  exactly the claims this is meant to release.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Refresh the test inventory in the phase guide\n\n* Docs: Keep main''s docs lockfile after the merge\n\nThe branch''s older lockfile won a hunk renovate had bumped on main, silently\ndowngrading qs. Nothing about this phase needs a docs dependency change.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Copy nginx''s spa.conf into the remote''s image\n\nmain moved the remotes'' SPA fallback into a shared /etc/nginx/spa.conf that every\nclient Dockerfile copies out of the base image, and the bundled nginx.conf includes it.\nThe new component''s Dockerfile was written before that and did not copy it, so its\ncontainer died at startup (\"open() /etc/nginx/spa.conf failed\"), which in turn kept\nclient-core''s nginx from resolving the remote''s upstream and took the whole e2e stack\ndown. Its tsconfig also still listed the tailwind config that this branch removed.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Answer the privacy and phase deletion requests\n\nEvery other phase service on main registers the SDK privacy endpoints, and core does not\nconsult a capability flag before calling them: it fans a request out to every phase type\nof the courses the subject is in, by base URL. A missing route is a 404, one failed\nsubrequest marks the whole request failed, and the student is mailed that their deletion\ndid not go through. Since the demo seed puts this phase on the full course, that applied\nto the existing privacy suite as much as to a real deployment.\n\n- privacy/: the export carries the subject''s own resource instances with their provider\n  and resource type joined in; the deletion removes them and leaves team-scoped\n  instances, which belong to the team, alone.\n- coursePhaseDeletion/: implements the handler prompt-sdk v1.3.1 added (main has it in\n  team_allocation so far). It matters more here than elsewhere, because the rows for a\n  deleted phase include the encrypted credentials of external systems. Deleted from the\n  leaves up so a weakened cascade cannot leave orphans, inside a bounded transaction.\n- Both are declared in /info, and the SDK is now on v1.3.1 like every other service.\n- /info''s health probe no longer pings the database without a deadline.\n\nNeither deletion touches the external resources: this phase never deletes them, so a\ngroup named after a student outlives their deletion request. That is now stated in the\nphase guide next to the rest of the delete semantics.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Align the new phase with main''s dependency bumps\n\nThe merge of main brought the toolchain and shared dependencies forward for\nevery existing service and remote, but the infrastructure setup phase was\nadded after that bump landed, so it stayed on the older Go toolchain,\nprompt-sdk, sentry-go, logrus and rspack versions.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Record an instance''s outcome on a context of its own\n\nOnly failClaimed detached its writes from the run''s context. The per-instance\nwrites did not, so when the run''s context expired the failure could not be\nrecorded either: createWithRetry returned the context error and the write that\nwas meant to carry it failed on the same dead context.\n\nEvery remaining instance then stayed in_progress, where a trigger answers 409\nand Retry refuses to touch it, leaving the phase unusable until the stale-claim\nsweeper ran 45 minutes later. All three terminal writes now go through a context\ndetached from the run.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop an unresponsive provider from consuming a run\n\nEvery provider built its HTTP client with no timeout, so a host that accepts the\nconnection and never answers (a firewall dropping the response, a hung TLS\nhandshake) held the request open until the run''s 30 minute context expired. With\nfive worker goroutines that turns one unreachable provider into a run that\nprovisions nothing and never reaches its own retry logic.\n\nProviders now share a client with a per-request timeout, and each CreateResource\nattempt gets a deadline of its own, because a resource with many members issues\none request per member and a per-request timeout alone does not bound the call.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Converge a re-trigger on the instances already there\n\nBoth instance uniqueness indexes excluded failed rows, so a failed instance was\ninvisible to them and a second trigger inserted another row for the same target\ninstead of retrying the one that was there. The phase then showed two rows for\none team with no way to tell them apart, and resetting either of them to pending\ncollided with the other: a raw 23505 left the retry endpoint answering 500 with\na Postgres constraint message.\n\nA phase whose instances went partial had the opposite problem. Those rows are in\nthe index but are not counted as non-terminal, so the trigger passed its guard,\ninserted nothing, queued nothing, and still answered 202 ''execution started''. A\nlecturer retrying a cohort that had not signed in yet got a success toast over a\nno-op, which is the first thing a real run runs into.\n\nA (config, target) pair now carries exactly one instance, enforced by the\ndatabase, and a trigger converges on it: create what is missing, requeue what\nfailed or came back partial, leave what was created alone. The endpoint returns\nwhat it did so a trigger with nothing to do says so.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Keep the permission mapping editable after a run\n\nEditing a config was refused outright once it had any instance that was not\nfailed. That covers partial, and an unmapped role is exactly what leaves a run\npartial: every provider turns a member whose role has no permission into a\nwarning. Since a retry re-reads the stored mapping, fixing a typo in it meant\ndeleting every instance of the config first and provisioning the whole\nconfiguration again.\n\nWhat the resource is called and where it lives still cannot change, because the\ninstance would keep pointing at the object that exists. The mapping is separated\nfrom that identity: it is editable as long as no instance of the config is\nqueued or running. The dialog names the locked fields and the card is badged so\nthe rule is visible before the lecturer types anything.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Tell a Rancher duplicate apart from a rejected binding\n\nRancher answers 422 for a duplicate binding and for a validation error alike, so\ntreating every 422 as ''already bound'' reported real rejections as success. An\nunknown roleTemplateId (a typo in the extra config is enough) left the project\ncreated, the instance marked created, and the student never granted access, with\nnothing in the UI to reveal it.\n\nUpstream failures now carry their status and the body''s error code as a typed\nerror instead of a message the caller greps for. Rancher accepts 409, or a 422\ncarrying NotUnique, as a duplicate and reports every other 422 as a member\nwarning. GitLab''s conflict check reads the same status instead of matching on\n''HTTP 409''.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop an unsaved semester tag from naming resources\n\nThe setup page filled the semester tag field from the parent course, so it read\nback a tag the phase had never saved. The server resolves {{semesterTag}} from\nthe phase row alone, so a lecturer who saw ''ios26'' in a saved-looking field got\na GitLab group called ''-ios-team-1'' (or ''ios-team-1'' where the provider trims\nthe separator), and nothing said why.\n\nThe course tag is now the field''s placeholder and an explicit ''use the course\ntag'' action, and the page says that provisioning is refused while nothing is\nsaved. The trigger enforces that: a name template or templated extra-config\nvalue that uses the placeholder is a 400 until the tag exists.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Say what each execution row is about\n\nA row showed its own UUID and eight characters of a team or participation id.\nFor an iPraktikum-sized course that is 90 rows of hex with no way to tell which\nteam failed, what was created for it, or which config it came from, and a failed\nrow has no external URL either.\n\nInstances now carry the two labels the list needs: the team or student name as\ncore resolved it, and the name the provider was last asked to create, both\nwritten by the worker so even a row that failed upstream says what it was. The\nlist joins its config in for the provider and resource kind, and the page adds\nstatus counts that double as filters plus a search over the names.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Report team members that cannot be resolved\n\nTeam member emails are resolved from this phase''s participations, but a tutor is\nassigned to a team at course level. A tutor who does not take part in the phase\nmissed the lookup and was dropped from the member list with no trace, so a\ntutor permission mapping quietly granted nobody anything and the instance still\nreported created. The same held for any member whose participation carried no\nemail address.\n\nTarget resolution now explains who it could not turn into a member, and the\nworker carries those warnings onto the instance: the resource is created, the\ninstance comes back partial, and the message names the people who were left out.\nResolving a tutor who is not a phase participant would need a course-level\nparticipation lookup that core does not expose, so the docs say to add them to\nthe phase instead.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Refuse the committed placeholder encryption key\n\nThe .env templates ship a key that decodes to exactly 32 bytes so a fresh\ncheckout starts, and the startup gate accepted it. A deployment created with\n''cp .env.template .env'' therefore came up clean while encrypting GitLab personal\naccess tokens, Slack bot tokens and Rancher secret keys with a key anyone can\nread out of the repository.\n\nThat value is now refused unless DEBUG=true, which is what local development and\nthe e2e stack run with, so only a real deployment is affected and it fails at\nstartup rather than silently.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Explain a Slack name conflict it cannot see into\n\nconversations.list returns a private channel only to an app that belongs to it,\nso a private channel a human created holds the name and never shows up. The\nlookup after a name_taken then failed with ''not found after name conflict'',\nwhich reads like a bug in PROMPT rather than the one thing a lecturer can act\non. No bot-token call reveals such a channel, so this is the platform''s limit,\nnot something the phase can work around.\n\nThe lookup now also lists public channels, not to adopt one (that would widen\nwho can read a team''s material) but so a name taken by a public channel is\nreported as exactly that. Both cases name the channel and say what to do, and\nthe limitation is documented next to the idempotency claim.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Only retry a provider failure that could succeed\n\nEvery provider error was retried three times with backoff, including the ones\nthat describe the request rather than the moment: a revoked token, an invalid\nname, a parent group id that does not exist. Across 90 instances at five workers\nthat turned one configuration mistake into minutes of provider load before the\nphase reported anything.\n\nAn upstream 4xx now fails the instance on the first attempt, with 408 and 429 as\nthe exceptions, while an error carrying no HTTP status (a dial failure, a\ntimeout, a parse error) is retried as before.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Stop the upsert dialogs from wiping what is typed\n\nBoth upsert dialogs seeded their form from an effect that depended on the array\nof available provider types. The parent rebuilds that array inline on every\nrender, so any re-render while the dialog was open (a window-focus refetch of the\nprovider list is enough) re-ran the effect and cleared the form, discarding a\nhalf-typed access token or an edited name template.\n\nThe form is seeded when the dialog opens, which is the only moment it should be.\n\nDeleting a resource config also invalidates the instances query now: the delete\ncascades to its instances in SQL, and the execution list went on rendering them\nwith live retry and delete buttons. The provider card already did this.\n\n* Infrastructure Setup: Close the smaller gaps from the review\n\nDeleting a provider config answered 400 for every failure, including a database\nerror, because the handler could not tell a rejected request from a broken one.\nUnknown provider types are now marked as validation failures and everything else\nanswers 500.\n\nThe permission mapping accepted any role string, but resolution only ever\nassigns ''student'' and ''tutor'', so a typo produced a row that reached nobody. The\ndialog offers those two (keeping whatever a saved config already names) and says\nthat keycloak and slack ignore the mapping entirely, which nothing had mentioned.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Maximilian Rapp <maximilian.rapp@tum.de>\nCo-authored-by: Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Josef Schmid <51161233+JGStyle@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqorl7CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAqL8QACsIvMwwqnjfJtbfhMM1nJFB\nisPYQzI5DIcfGMc8X1hQj4tnogK87p2kKUHVeoTMUGs69cCplZfDSc3ecp0tlkaM\nw7BJVFJ+WLlM/dE6/tKBL/jDKGWvyGnPQLh39Fw+lbCrF+nKxWXb/Y5XDvqJEapp\nkdj49MQ1g6qW42mtHwt5san2xqV8StlvjXsIebg2TlJM302bVBW0MJ8RwJCxwEeP\nvXrsbVPlZh9DNPExZU4RBT8nUWZY6X3Fp8kCRlMUs3Fzlyu+4dDIMOwnTkZ5tBPT\naQ1nTXh9BL48hxPhxeQGUld/cawYkH9y1cTWJNMXFXYDyfCPiK7p9Xt8V+d5hl7z\nT0Wf7Zrjb8KyzzAyKfEwbyIFtCmjcv2mzhPdjev/t3VRcm7N41rtoydws8DkHdEP\n7RgScAjtqqP6igiAUkG/epjWLUquO83RhkBSXQfZa2rrZC6AcusVShT0YqZ2JcEH\n3dsXE+KCYuql3ntmCxngiWlR7eXBRem9y8SZuV1NvMV8uoWmAuNyBeoN2z6UP9OV\nGR8NyqcW9RndmMX0tD40nANcfmC5ZgyN2fustZOUQHH5FWryEhKDD65QFkPHmzL9\n44O6glDjvXAVX6JXa5ghQVTepP0fU4vXRpI2Wyi+ITK6penrzuvFt5evzVLMLQkk\nquVXe6CDlMBfPKVFJDH/\n=jVe4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T14:06:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKGI3N2MxOWE1YzFhNzhlMTMyZGVhZjIzNTZjN2Y2ODlmZGYxMWMwMDI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961","sha":"0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961"}],"sha":"b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b77c19a5c1a78e132deaf2356c7f689fdf11c002"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:02:00Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:02:00Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Team Allocation: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2180)\n\n* Capture team allocation mutations into the central audit log\n\nThe service built three sibling API groups on the router, so a single\nmiddleware could only ever cover one of them. Collapse them onto the existing\napi group, register the shared audit middleware before the course phase\nsubgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited team allocation actions\n\nName the routes whose derived label is wrong, from \"Created save\" for\npublishing a TEASE allocation to \"Deleted course phase\" for a module that only\ndeletes its own data. Scope the phase copy to its target and keep core''s\ncopyability probe, which posts a copy of a phase onto itself, out of the log.\n\n* Cover the team allocation audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the action labels and the explicit copy event.\nThe TEASE routes are the case that matters: they sat on their own group, so\nbefore the consolidation no single middleware reached them.","tree":{"sha":"00ef4b1ce80016d814fdc2d33d438f4762c57072","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/00ef4b1ce80016d814fdc2d33d438f4762c57072"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961","verification":{"payload":"tree 00ef4b1ce80016d814fdc2d33d438f4762c57072\nparent 6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789045320 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789045320 +0100\n\nTeam Allocation: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2180)\n\n* Capture team allocation mutations into the central audit log\n\nThe service built three sibling API groups on the router, so a single\nmiddleware could only ever cover one of them. Collapse them onto the existing\napi group, register the shared audit middleware before the course phase\nsubgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited team allocation actions\n\nName the routes whose derived label is wrong, from \"Created save\" for\npublishing a TEASE allocation to \"Deleted course phase\" for a module that only\ndeletes its own data. Scope the phase copy to its target and keep core''s\ncopyability probe, which posts a copy of a phase onto itself, out of the log.\n\n* Cover the team allocation audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the action labels and the explicit copy event.\nThe TEASE routes are the case that matters: they sat on their own group, so\nbefore the consolidation no single middleware reached them.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoqpICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZHYQAAIG1gowVTWh/U5U1emSZAum\nNr7uhdt+Kdyve7zJiMMZ0RbtszTWf+JU+GkRbHOnMdP3s1/91ndfLDQv1hr4iC1t\nWct+8vXNnlZOPdd7iDYjqzxGqFWbmkFcZRxdSDxRk1QJV+5ykCvIzaQtPHNn0GHa\najf9WZ76EO7qChETzuukv5BHV+PWwJxO4sUw9Y+lxQDeht5Nn/I4h2Qz+k9///nv\nAf53dB9sNY00w+//XMNXkeXbYCNEgCJ6qYRwo2wPRJfrJY1H9YzkOOIFvSMFBEyC\nXxEZbA1P6kfXnUgEcyydrPIPx2sOYVlvXSjECn0Sw6rL7RnaZA08kHWT/IYYDVv4\nv45149dYTo1VvsfKKJerAtTAiRfLWqqaBbbQNkb9bgmyVVdP2/KO954tCk9Yr5NT\na2AD842ARWlABJTfka9pYCCfYPq73TZ8H9pKfWJ/4gD5CTwjU8WHR8+6R3Bu4Dp0\nw6sdP0QquA/OPDbc01ePHNlMNXPlQenfIq2HNDmw3wlL2H+nBLGDUQVWG43ydqLD\nbyf3gRpRb/4NR33qTfKtOhdv+qs1l4V86E20oQGXqekITmyxxIGYHlMGQsco2IDb\n9GwSPiGsBaJ+xnYS7K38ypGFhIc/LE5MmqGCC8swBvEF/RWZ/RYZmjKk01ZtiQ6K\n5/pPACbWYRqHgfAXDv+H\n=JPKO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T13:02:01Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKDBhYTcxMmUyNDRjNTlmNzJiYjRkZmEyMGZiZjE1ZDA3YTg3MzQ5NjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573","sha":"6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573"}],"sha":"0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/0aa712e244c59f72bb4dfa20fbf15d07a8734961"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:35Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:35Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Presentation: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2178)\n\n* Capture presentation mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the presentation API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, label the copy route, and advertise the audit log\ncapability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited presentation actions\n\nEvery mutating route here derives a misleading label, from \"Created categorie\"\nto \"Created submit\", so all of them are named. Scope the phase copy to its\ntarget and keep core''s copyability probe out of the log: the probe posts a copy\nof a phase onto itself and runs the real handler.\n\n* Cover the presentation audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order and all twenty action labels, plus the explicit\ncopy event and the suppressed probe.\n\n* Stop the copyability probe from wiping a presentation phase\n\nCore asks a phase service whether it supports copying by posting a copy request\nwhose source and target are the same phase, and the caller''s token makes that\nrequest run the real handler. Copying in place clears the target first, so\nopening the copy course dialog deleted the phase''s own slots, presentations and\nuploaded material, restoring only its configuration.","tree":{"sha":"002aa6088969c2d54f207d959e330b46a237e15e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/002aa6088969c2d54f207d959e330b46a237e15e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573","verification":{"payload":"tree 002aa6088969c2d54f207d959e330b46a237e15e\nparent de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789045295 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789045295 +0100\n\nPresentation: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2178)\n\n* Capture presentation mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the presentation API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, label the copy route, and advertise the audit log\ncapability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited presentation actions\n\nEvery mutating route here derives a misleading label, from \"Created categorie\"\nto \"Created submit\", so all of them are named. Scope the phase copy to its\ntarget and keep core''s copyability probe out of the log: the probe posts a copy\nof a phase onto itself and runs the real handler.\n\n* Cover the presentation audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order and all twenty action labels, plus the explicit\ncopy event and the suppressed probe.\n\n* Stop the copyability probe from wiping a presentation phase\n\nCore asks a phase service whether it supports copying by posting a copy request\nwhose source and target are the same phase, and the caller''s token makes that\nrequest run the real handler. Copying in place clears the target first, so\nopening the copy course dialog deleted the phase''s own slots, presentations and\nuploaded material, restoring only its configuration.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoqovCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApY8QAAYd7w86Pv6aZeCB0jgBI3CW\n7n4whCH2Yh2FGxDOm+bqG/g1PpBqcZJGSCnsx+DTjltwToOh1winsYpm8/XKuZzo\n200KcppLxgR5AYYMWMwhdBnExiBhcrFlRq/uvWijop87adNOek7K2nb3QXkZ9bJz\nzWBAiGEgGZe492UoAeNVx8u6r4mL5qBQ0gCIuJ2sR0ZakosamzMTiJveB7M7j2WX\n/UHdmqeAml3FMbdCHnWkx6RJQBwTQZWIV04KXwJbi9Rjc4dG6Ilu3a3jfBD/cY11\nVDuvaTtvp3Bj3KU27PQxBUWJj8yP3UsrzibxTraGBDJX/0Rcbf0yE8nm/hKumgXp\n4MS0W/9MF5XfNTLom9hvN5Eovrztfqn2lNeMx8SnKdv88OlM5izTYWG9CN9Q+gqB\nsn9DDMiqsT2MvMcfhz/mUmKM2rux5stb0n33FDTfbbykuvpah/l+Ho3GBBAVHZWb\nT71mDHCRQgstXgCzNrVj+34hpLYNpzvWpsqU9GsKAinIMoq0jnOjcwlaYqr37FlV\nR5X5MbK175JmyCuEuaEsWOVBlS5CTF+xGqMLIBdkD7Mi+RlBw79mRqGdsMDNY5lQ\nDfelCndD/5FskQbXoQ0OFO4IdamJiaPAkQQPzeYQZxpxYKB7oNE0KzbreLNMLPOx\nhCA9n2gOguKglb2Yh6mr\n=H/2t\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T13:01:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKDZhNGEyZmQzNzhjMTk3Zjk3OTliNWRhNzBkYzZlNGQzODdhODY1NzM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7","sha":"de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7"}],"sha":"6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/6a4a2fd378c197f9799b5da70dc6e4d387a86573"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:21Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:21Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Interview: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2177)\n\n* Capture interview mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the interview API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited interview actions\n\nLabel the two routes whose derived name is meaningless, and scope the phase\ncopy to its target: the copy route sits outside the course phase group, so an\nautomatically captured event would never reach the course audit log.\n\n* Cover the interview audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the action labels and the explicit copy event\nwithout a database or Keycloak.\n\n* Keep the copyability probe out of the audit log\n\nCore detects whether a phase service supports copying by posting a copy\nrequest whose source and target are the same phase. That request runs the real\nhandler, so without suppressing it every copy dialog would append a course\nscoped entry claiming the phase had been copied onto itself.\n\n* Keep the empty phase copy out of the audit log\n\nInterview slots and reviews are tied to the phase they were created for, so a\ncopy carries nothing over. Recording it would put positive evidence in the\ntarget course''s log for work that never happened, so the request is suppressed\ninstead, which also covers core''s copyability probe.","tree":{"sha":"e8f653c997262c77777ab563c2b89b92aef99632","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/e8f653c997262c77777ab563c2b89b92aef99632"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7","verification":{"payload":"tree e8f653c997262c77777ab563c2b89b92aef99632\nparent d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789045281 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789045281 +0100\n\nInterview: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2177)\n\n* Capture interview mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the interview API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited interview actions\n\nLabel the two routes whose derived name is meaningless, and scope the phase\ncopy to its target: the copy route sits outside the course phase group, so an\nautomatically captured event would never reach the course audit log.\n\n* Cover the interview audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the action labels and the explicit copy event\nwithout a database or Keycloak.\n\n* Keep the copyability probe out of the audit log\n\nCore detects whether a phase service supports copying by posting a copy\nrequest whose source and target are the same phase. That request runs the real\nhandler, so without suppressing it every copy dialog would append a course\nscoped entry claiming the phase had been copied onto itself.\n\n* Keep the empty phase copy out of the audit log\n\nInterview slots and reviews are tied to the phase they were created for, so a\ncopy carries nothing over. Recording it would put positive evidence in the\ntarget course''s log for work that never happened, so the request is suppressed\ninstead, which also covers core''s copyability probe.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoqohCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8IAQAD+i6ebHntotUSSFDfXdse2B\ntYLhcCg3x288yVSMOkY2cDBm+TrDjjqDFNQsQ99InJjbU9alB0x4HAkJZD3u48uy\nmCIKzkAHztqm8S5l80RJzryVKbAgoAcTrEiTvZr0ABb//NdRaduTXMKSIyLNXdcw\nShEeoIRNqZZ72RP3B42As2tD073ItKvWoF+6Lhp5FPjbX6g66JenRNh806y84V4l\nwDB/GVgFhHR3dMybTR52Q8jMys6pslgECbDe1SFyPnTPoJDbh1Uq6Xc9yVZ6xuuu\nXSfMMZfQTn2m8OLmtesvdeoL2LmlD+smBAsM7COu6ppwAIickAr6AsKLxLdLOgUK\nHd5vbhE8bL+6v4tp8Uc7tdBTQbEtLUc3fFT2EJfHPGhEC0aS+P4PNZ7DHrZZ855E\ni+qU0/Ypwx1TfX0UAWbtvM4b20wxLXjndsEwIXvI1HharT8JGrsPO5pOBNkKc8Ua\ntVPb75go8wF+CZlUvRneI92Kz2MM1KjkzFCSkF2NCJDPv0CrJsWjtzw89ePkRkdw\nJTFZ6IQG49lLpVGAC2/UKKymgVDU1YJOks0meIvC8hyxsHkdmx+Aqp6QUu/X0NUg\no0sZolw4zvvMCB+PSrESB1XwnS3ahzumTLdyRIHfkZJ7nRnrvKYsl53wh1uBrL9u\nPilI1PJDAywJpt59Hr+S\n=NFBl\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T13:01:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKGRlODBkYTYwMGJkYTdlNTYzOWY1YzY5NzdjN2QxZWQ2OGUyYmEyZjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4","sha":"d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4"}],"sha":"de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/de80da600bda7e5639f5c6977c7d1ed68e2ba2f7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:03Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:01:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Example: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2176)\n\n* Capture example service mutations into the central audit log\n\nCollapse the two sibling API groups into one parent so a single middleware\ncovers every route, register the shared audit middleware on it before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited phase copy\n\nThe copy route sits outside the course phase group, so an automatically\ncaptured event carries no phase and would never reach the course audit log.\n\n* Cover the example service audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the copy label and the explicit copy event\nwithout a database or Keycloak.\n\n* Keep an unscoped copy request out of the audit log\n\nA phase copy request carries no required fields, so a blank body would pin an\nappend-only entry to the nil course phase. Share the action label between the\nroute and the explicit event so filtering by action finds both.\n\n* Keep the copyability probe out of the audit log\n\nCore detects whether a phase service supports copying by posting a copy\nrequest whose source and target are the same phase. That request runs the real\nhandler, so without suppressing it every copy dialog would append an entry\nclaiming the phase had been copied onto itself.\n\n* Describe the copy action constant accurately\n\nA denied copy never reaches the audit log: without a course phase in the path\nthe SDK auth middleware rejects a course lecturer with a 400, which the\nmiddleware ignores. The label serves the automatic entry instead.","tree":{"sha":"ce8ce2229fa48fe5ec749775ae8b24db065ee7a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/ce8ce2229fa48fe5ec749775ae8b24db065ee7a0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4","verification":{"payload":"tree ce8ce2229fa48fe5ec749775ae8b24db065ee7a0\nparent 621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789045263 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789045263 +0100\n\nExample: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2176)\n\n* Capture example service mutations into the central audit log\n\nCollapse the two sibling API groups into one parent so a single middleware\ncovers every route, register the shared audit middleware on it before the\ncourse phase subgroup exists, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name and scope the audited phase copy\n\nThe copy route sits outside the course phase group, so an automatically\ncaptured event carries no phase and would never reach the course audit log.\n\n* Cover the example service audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order, the copy label and the explicit copy event\nwithout a database or Keycloak.\n\n* Keep an unscoped copy request out of the audit log\n\nA phase copy request carries no required fields, so a blank body would pin an\nappend-only entry to the nil course phase. Share the action label between the\nroute and the explicit event so filtering by action finds both.\n\n* Keep the copyability probe out of the audit log\n\nCore detects whether a phase service supports copying by posting a copy\nrequest whose source and target are the same phase. That request runs the real\nhandler, so without suppressing it every copy dialog would append an entry\nclaiming the phase had been copied onto itself.\n\n* Describe the copy action constant accurately\n\nA denied copy never reaches the audit log: without a course phase in the path\nthe SDK auth middleware rejects a course lecturer with a 400, which the\nmiddleware ignores. The label serves the automatic entry instead.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoqoPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAcwoQAJJumkJ07+I9bFYd69E0WYq6\nFZa52MaWe6J59WLoidfnMI3GymJUWjksCsysrN6uJmTDIHNLNmv14doSt2I0Tg3C\nt/b3Y3ncoO6i4QyOLgWQlki8QH+IFwQAFya2vZnaNB8HciNImPZCRenlQeXft9+0\nGDygIRr1cPiFYZnjdIn3sfLllb8i05qZFRVqTlPB5zRjU0mNVz3aS68zCLLPR2YF\nkORYnoZr7VLN5IvIMjoyaH27RJlhdsa+XOEwU44Hx//GG3Dnr/4I0ibzVonooW0F\nvrnwkjKSwYWN5A+WkffOE4yhvo1JEQCK0WoquEe8clC65zGtYqnXRY5PnVAhHnpJ\nEdK7KoaQNVt78JZImFmTqNuv33wQIVUSGSGApLWedulBJjI4lvBIR/NBC6Wfkiw6\n/vIF6SAqVahe3g+3ERSnuDh6Bcp1PnrwFNL3IA+3MfpA+7TKQMQVJZFcEew4WxA7\nOsMAUKr3ESKCRUIZfHinT54y4AE8FWkcWmRXQryRM7AGQWv/tO9ax2fbhr9/QkX3\nIelA3TnKcQSnVAIl7Mu/pYZu34VdMKafHr6NoAeR6qGBI7Mz6+L445P7h9bL9n+3\n0GARyDT1mzKbNJWqgrdcORwPwEMns3nbzpbawA1hD9K9267ReLYNQXcgEQGlltm7\nOgeb9+MQ8ZzVI4wKQ3ju\n=ETrs\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T13:01:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKGQ2YTBjZGRiNGFiN2Y5MmQ2ZmVlZWFhYmE5MGMxN2Y5ZTM3N2VhZjQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8","sha":"621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8"}],"sha":"d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/d6a0cddb4ab7f92d6feeeaaba90c17f9e377eaf4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T11:23:27Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T11:23:27Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Certificate: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2175)\n\n* Capture certificate mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the certificate API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup is created, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name the audited certificate actions\n\nLabel the three configuration writes, and record an explicit event when staff\ndownload another student''s certificate: the generator routes are reads, which\nthe automatic capture ignores.\n\n* Cover the certificate audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order and every action label without a database or\nKeycloak: a request without a bearer token is denied by the SDK auth\nmiddleware, which the audit middleware records.\n\n* Name the audited student in the certificate download event\n\nStore the student''s name alongside the identifier so the course audit log is\nreadable without cross-referencing core.","tree":{"sha":"d388214ed0ac3f364622a010a434c2315b24bdde","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/d388214ed0ac3f364622a010a434c2315b24bdde"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8","verification":{"payload":"tree d388214ed0ac3f364622a010a434c2315b24bdde\nparent abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789039407 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789039407 +0100\n\nCertificate: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2175)\n\n* Capture certificate mutations into the central audit log\n\nRegister the shared audit middleware on the certificate API group before the\ncourse phase subgroup is created, and advertise the audit log capability.\n\n* Name the audited certificate actions\n\nLabel the three configuration writes, and record an explicit event when staff\ndownload another student''s certificate: the generator routes are reads, which\nthe automatic capture ignores.\n\n* Cover the certificate audit wiring\n\nPin the middleware chain order and every action label without a database or\nKeycloak: a request without a bearer token is denied by the SDK auth\nmiddleware, which the audit middleware records.\n\n* Name the audited student in the certificate download event\n\nStore the student''s name alongside the identifier so the course audit log is\nreadable without cross-referencing core.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqopMvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5zoQAD6MMlnh59o4EaxYmVYIaq6M\nPQGVIRjgGtEmdWGAJeUrV66C6OLkO0HDbr0xJBgYKYDfuUGD6Ysgw7UTtgVc82Wv\nUWNtigLyT9+15+4yFvoTksEU23Qyw5/5t1Q+AXMy5R/X+PbTNfMJtK2ACNJSHn4+\nlXIfqUAyyVz0LBEeu7KqwDFg99RL/n8WEP6fqSq0zPGgotqF5czD9zS0xZK2UNUO\nqrbfAMPqj/OhjQMt4jnWquKcVl8/PePSMo1Q5aluZ+OePCJ0aXmqvMBx2IHKWNT6\nLyKn+LYHT1FkYDaNGXZekYNCoEc8++8VIZ8RC6HM1YwI/2/Kc6LYqlsJuX7GQvqg\nFU0s3UDv1u5aUPAdp5Wa6moD2QNaHd47PZWWFKuVs7FM98zNa3jKcPWFBiaimOFd\ngav9kBMsxUmkMXICjrWDm5TWvmzBeZtJpZ77KcEQPnq2vUwLY4/QVK344ZS1IRry\nFyKK0GX3SuPZXn2mVToqW2Gm7QE/IgnD1ZjfxLdwk2yUdyAVqQapJLGnhSd2ZQvD\nmVrZ8uhnL0iSvF6cd9khcRQGdtB/e9UycX8kmjtF18mZwtqngoikp4VN1lJfG+mo\nXM9zXuHK+cWanGh2yLlOvC+DiS3Sm9xNiuId2cGHRZG0CvtA7M7WIyqrhXZ1ApJ7\nuHSPPMDlrEYPra2ISdod\n=bcwj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T11:23:28Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKDYyMWY3NjQyZTdiOWY4NDE2NDQ2NGI4OWMwYmU2Mjk5Njk2Mzc5YTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66","sha":"abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66"}],"sha":"621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/621f7642e7b9f84164464b89c0be6299696379a8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:14:14Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:14:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Core: Provision per-service audit ingest keys and forward the audit environment (#2171)\n\n* Core: Forward per-service audit ingest keys to the phase containers\n\naudit.NewCoreSink reads the reporting service''s secret from AUDIT_INGEST_KEY,\nbut the dev and prod compose files forwarded the AUDIT_* settings to\nserver-core only, so no phase container had a key and every phase sink would\nhave come back nil.\n\nForwarding the bare AUDIT_INGEST_KEY to every container would hand all\nservices the same secret, which defeats the point of a per-service key. Each\nservice now gets its own AUDIT_INGEST_KEY_<SERVICE> variable, mapped onto the\nname the SDK reads. The AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS comment block lists every reporting\nservice name so the core-side value can be assembled from it.\n\n* Testing: Provision audit ingest keys for the e2e phase services\n\nThe e2e stack enabled the audit log on core but configured no ingest keys, so\na phase reporting events would have been rejected with a 401. Core now\nreceives AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS and every phase container gets its own key.\n\nThe values live in the committed e2e/.env.e2e alongside the other test-only\ncredentials; they authenticate nothing outside the throwaway stack.\n\n* Deployment: Write the per-service audit ingest keys into .env.prod\n\nEach phase service''s key is read from its own repository secret, next to the\nexisting AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS entry, so the compose files can map it onto the\nAUDIT_INGEST_KEY the service reads.\n\n* Documentation: Describe audit ingest key provisioning per phase service\n\nThe admin guide now walks through generating one key per service, listing them\nin core''s AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS under the name each service reports on its info\nendpoint, and handing each phase its own key. Includes the reported-name to\ncompose-variable mapping, and notes that intro-course is deployed from its own\nrepository and that a service without a key simply does not report.\n\n* Core: Tighten the audit ingest key plumbing after review\n\n- default the new compose interpolations to an empty string, so a developer\n  whose .env predates this change does not get seven unset-variable warnings\n  on every compose invocation\n- teach the phase scaffolder about the key: it copies the server-example\n  compose block, so a new phase referenced an AUDIT_INGEST_KEY_<NAME>_SERVER\n  variable that no template defined\n- say plainly that host mode exports one key to every server, that the\n  minimal and extern.prod stacks carry no audit configuration, and that the\n  e2e stack pins AUDIT_ENABLED rather than forwarding it\n- describe the e2e keys as provisioning: no phase wires a sink yet, so\n  nothing reports until the per-service issues land","tree":{"sha":"28fe85c2c2b5bed712fef0944d647e0b7b6453e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/28fe85c2c2b5bed712fef0944d647e0b7b6453e8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66","verification":{"payload":"tree 28fe85c2c2b5bed712fef0944d647e0b7b6453e8\nparent b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789035254 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789035254 +0100\n\nCore: Provision per-service audit ingest keys and forward the audit environment (#2171)\n\n* Core: Forward per-service audit ingest keys to the phase containers\n\naudit.NewCoreSink reads the reporting service''s secret from AUDIT_INGEST_KEY,\nbut the dev and prod compose files forwarded the AUDIT_* settings to\nserver-core only, so no phase container had a key and every phase sink would\nhave come back nil.\n\nForwarding the bare AUDIT_INGEST_KEY to every container would hand all\nservices the same secret, which defeats the point of a per-service key. Each\nservice now gets its own AUDIT_INGEST_KEY_<SERVICE> variable, mapped onto the\nname the SDK reads. The AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS comment block lists every reporting\nservice name so the core-side value can be assembled from it.\n\n* Testing: Provision audit ingest keys for the e2e phase services\n\nThe e2e stack enabled the audit log on core but configured no ingest keys, so\na phase reporting events would have been rejected with a 401. Core now\nreceives AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS and every phase container gets its own key.\n\nThe values live in the committed e2e/.env.e2e alongside the other test-only\ncredentials; they authenticate nothing outside the throwaway stack.\n\n* Deployment: Write the per-service audit ingest keys into .env.prod\n\nEach phase service''s key is read from its own repository secret, next to the\nexisting AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS entry, so the compose files can map it onto the\nAUDIT_INGEST_KEY the service reads.\n\n* Documentation: Describe audit ingest key provisioning per phase service\n\nThe admin guide now walks through generating one key per service, listing them\nin core''s AUDIT_INGEST_KEYS under the name each service reports on its info\nendpoint, and handing each phase its own key. Includes the reported-name to\ncompose-variable mapping, and notes that intro-course is deployed from its own\nrepository and that a service without a key simply does not report.\n\n* Core: Tighten the audit ingest key plumbing after review\n\n- default the new compose interpolations to an empty string, so a developer\n  whose .env predates this change does not get seven unset-variable warnings\n  on every compose invocation\n- teach the phase scaffolder about the key: it copies the server-example\n  compose block, so a new phase referenced an AUDIT_INGEST_KEY_<NAME>_SERVER\n  variable that no template defined\n- say plainly that host mode exports one key to every server, that the\n  minimal and extern.prod stacks carry no audit configuration, and that the\n  e2e stack pins AUDIT_ENABLED rather than forwarding it\n- describe the e2e keys as provisioning: no phase wires a sink yet, so\n  nothing reports until the per-service issues land","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqooL2CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0ZAQAH412aFTJFtH9TnfSzXRM5Ry\neywpQ8ud2pu4unHomXEbxcjsAQAeJntPkCF9As+LugLBNShzFPI/otibLo33gYN2\ngoCU2P3ZsH5mkPMoxP7liq8jCyxr4mZOp/NUYIzYQWU8DX9BMHdlNc1s9D8W/H25\n/BXpwNvC/3ZzkDx9GSje1urcqOozbFMQaQZ8dzgJiRw4asaNImzrDSLZpP8aBM2X\ne9uUkL0gk27T2aL4SFWlR0OG4tFupFcLwnQyxEYzB1BMdKWKeunQhVZrQmQXareV\ndzRSbnyvWzG1dBSsIPTYHDGyzd1o3UXjTGTejAoN2u7MrpUw/jC5dpFlIc2kmKsk\nUknZDBNbh+eM2EYGDF3Sb003JreU/lhg053yDKO4bQYNZk4rjY5//cGokqQQjFDR\nIcWJgbJ9Hu+AgU6oiIIqF/iKIEtY4rHVbnDUUl5cMHwAKwRf0+23xQe/VhmgO0pw\nMEuudr/jNl5a7CCLg0MbhGSRHc1F3DBd8yrABHP2j1pV8Z5Vz3E9izSIRvSwxdtJ\nfs5LFHN8yVS+FwoiNj04gGJXQQU9lRAG7xH4jqT8gCiGFs3afgnPUvH8fpVpKHyq\nA5lq2akiUKh59lxB+cr4I6lhB328OVmU8pAslYkJiUthm2kZ9a3WAfNfz65qi+6u\nIl9PbObn7xwlKo2cZr5Q\n=/FUo\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T10:14:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKGFiZDQzNDE3YzkzMDc1MWVmNmEwZmVlODlkYmVkNzQ5MThlMWRiNjY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e","sha":"b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e"}],"sha":"abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/abd43417c930751ef6a0fee89dbed74918e1db66"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:13:54Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:13:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Assessment: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2172)\n\n* Assessment: Report audit events to the central audit log\n\nRegisters the prompt-sdk audit capture middleware on the API group, so every\nmutating request that succeeds or is denied reaches core''s audit log.\n\nThe Use call sits between the api group and coursePhaseApi on purpose: gin\nsnapshots the middleware chain when a route or subgroup is registered, so\nregistering it any later would silently capture none of the phase routes.\n\nNewCoreSink returns nil unless AUDIT_ENABLED is set and an ingest key is\nconfigured, and Middleware is then a pass-through, so this is inert until an\nadministrator switches auditing on. The service reports the toggle as the\naudit.log capability on its info endpoint.\n\nPhase copy is labelled through a zero-width group because the SDK registrar\nowns the POST /copy route and exposes no per-route middleware slot.\n\n* Assessment: Name the audit actions that auto-derive badly\n\nThe middleware derives a label from method and path, which reads wrong for\nseveral routes: the completion endpoints are upserts rather than creates, and\nDELETE /completed/course-participation/:id would have been recorded as\n''Deleted course participation''.\n\nLabels the assessment and evaluation completion routes, the schema mutations,\nand the result release and reminder endpoints.\n\n* Assessment: Label denied requests too, and name two more upserts\n\nA role denial aborts with 401 only after KeycloakMiddleware has put the token\nuser in the context, so an actor resolves, outcomeForStatus classifies the\nrequest as denied, and the event is recorded. A Describe placed after the auth\nmiddleware never runs on exactly those rows, so the denial, the most\nsecurity-relevant entry in the log, kept the derived label: a CourseEditor\nrefused at /config/reminders/send was recorded as ''Created send''.\n\nDescribe only sets a context key and has no Next, so moving it ahead of the\nauth middleware is behaviourally inert for the allowed path and fixes the\ndenied one. The copy group already had this ordering by construction.\n\nAlso labels POST /evaluation and POST /category-assessment, which are upserts\nthat would otherwise claim a creation when they update.\n\n* Scope the assessment phase copy and silence the autosave routes\n\nThe copy route sits outside :coursePhaseID, so an automatically captured event\ncarries no phase and never reaches the course audit log. Record it explicitly\nagainst the target phase, and keep core''s copyability probe, which posts a copy\nof a phase onto itself and runs the real handler, out of the log entirely.\n\nThe grading form posts on every score selection and the evaluation form on every\nfield change, with no debounce, so auditing those two routes would bury the log.\nWorse, the SDK caps deliveries in flight at 64 and drops the overflow with only\na warning, so the flood would open gaps that look exactly like nothing having\nhappened. The completion transitions carry the auditable decision and stay.\n\nAlso names the configuration write, which derived as \"Updated config\".","tree":{"sha":"05ef062dcb98489f4e1e7a663ca80ba25a73ff1d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/05ef062dcb98489f4e1e7a663ca80ba25a73ff1d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e","verification":{"payload":"tree 05ef062dcb98489f4e1e7a663ca80ba25a73ff1d\nparent 32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789035234 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789035234 +0100\n\nAssessment: Report audit events to the central audit log (#2172)\n\n* Assessment: Report audit events to the central audit log\n\nRegisters the prompt-sdk audit capture middleware on the API group, so every\nmutating request that succeeds or is denied reaches core''s audit log.\n\nThe Use call sits between the api group and coursePhaseApi on purpose: gin\nsnapshots the middleware chain when a route or subgroup is registered, so\nregistering it any later would silently capture none of the phase routes.\n\nNewCoreSink returns nil unless AUDIT_ENABLED is set and an ingest key is\nconfigured, and Middleware is then a pass-through, so this is inert until an\nadministrator switches auditing on. The service reports the toggle as the\naudit.log capability on its info endpoint.\n\nPhase copy is labelled through a zero-width group because the SDK registrar\nowns the POST /copy route and exposes no per-route middleware slot.\n\n* Assessment: Name the audit actions that auto-derive badly\n\nThe middleware derives a label from method and path, which reads wrong for\nseveral routes: the completion endpoints are upserts rather than creates, and\nDELETE /completed/course-participation/:id would have been recorded as\n''Deleted course participation''.\n\nLabels the assessment and evaluation completion routes, the schema mutations,\nand the result release and reminder endpoints.\n\n* Assessment: Label denied requests too, and name two more upserts\n\nA role denial aborts with 401 only after KeycloakMiddleware has put the token\nuser in the context, so an actor resolves, outcomeForStatus classifies the\nrequest as denied, and the event is recorded. A Describe placed after the auth\nmiddleware never runs on exactly those rows, so the denial, the most\nsecurity-relevant entry in the log, kept the derived label: a CourseEditor\nrefused at /config/reminders/send was recorded as ''Created send''.\n\nDescribe only sets a context key and has no Next, so moving it ahead of the\nauth middleware is behaviourally inert for the allowed path and fixes the\ndenied one. The copy group already had this ordering by construction.\n\nAlso labels POST /evaluation and POST /category-assessment, which are upserts\nthat would otherwise claim a creation when they update.\n\n* Scope the assessment phase copy and silence the autosave routes\n\nThe copy route sits outside :coursePhaseID, so an automatically captured event\ncarries no phase and never reaches the course audit log. Record it explicitly\nagainst the target phase, and keep core''s copyability probe, which posts a copy\nof a phase onto itself and runs the real handler, out of the log entirely.\n\nThe grading form posts on every score selection and the evaluation form on every\nfield change, with no debounce, so auditing those two routes would bury the log.\nWorse, the SDK caps deliveries in flight at 64 and drops the overflow with only\na warning, so the flood would open gaps that look exactly like nothing having\nhappened. The completion transitions carry the auditable decision and stay.\n\nAlso names the configuration write, which derived as \"Updated config\".","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqooLiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA66QQAGVDcRqtT1grhmijf/s40UXp\n5cXDnB9hCtSefS2foFE+P6/u+Nho+FW1WVW6/6xNpL/JitPsymDdiCifU2aw2q4S\nnXE+sEN4CM3qp8sAeI91A38HxHCl1qxQ0DWYSW1BhhA3uXlu3hk12h76nL4G0Mt2\nFWXKrlLS5FhFOiNf2aL8ObCMws6SXuSiKVr/I02v/ExWFPsj84seqUz3o9cAxi0S\nmjTKsNg4e2sj6v1QpAyvsMVgHyLYYMi+5Sj1nvJzNa7Mhui36tgqNymcuQ9IZG9w\nklt2s87mkpp+hunikR2H97jp/NOp2TyVrFSo+gwdX1gWuudA8e37QW4FjYQQ4X67\no0rQEF0KbdiXqK0p9fz2gkL7CZBAvGI8WAFzOavtYJseIQgWawB+e1pWWZbQw0gq\nAaqjohKsVawcWrjnshj3A0+R4HQ33ah3iy/b8vV5/fg9GVc05PWdkOuWSLP5GeNw\ntB1l2uVuTfWoPODJcADofnZNA8OMYA90GfvNYbHkn19xOX0GW9iQjuYI70o+OIyw\nzs3TUFgBoyMIp7jLiMZjNHoIPLfCyNWlQzzNDL+msLbdg+9SdNrHjqhVR2ynDeI3\ngXIjSJfHk1FsoGdokG7fPyQ/nSmRohF/utMw3B2hMw6EgZhVJCogezGW7HiO18Zs\nd0J0YETgCC1NXx6chgEw\n=3xPa\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T10:13:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e","node_id":"C_kwDONRrZeNoAKGI0OGVjNDM1MDM4YTQ5NDBkZTg4MzhkZGRjN2QyZjUzNzBiYzhkNGU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/prompt-edu/prompt/commit/32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a","sha":"32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a"}],"sha":"b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/b48ec435038a4940de8838dddc7d2f5370bc8d4e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/53191688?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rappm","id":53191688,"login":"rappm","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTkxNjg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rappm","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/commits/32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:06:56Z","email":"53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Maximilian Rapp"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T10:06:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"General: Fix the open Dependabot and CodeQL security alerts (#2186)\n\n* General: Stop logging the database password on service startup\n\nThe assessment, team allocation and self team allocation services logged the\nfull Postgres DSN, which embeds DB_PASSWORD, so the credential appeared in\nplain text whenever the log level was debug. Core logged the same unsanitised\nDSN.\n\nApply the pattern the interview and example services already use: hoist the\nDB_* lookups to package-level variables and log only host, port, database,\nuser and sslmode, so the password no longer reaches a logging call.\n\n* Dependencies: Patch the vulnerable transitive npm dependencies\n\nUpgrade the transitive packages behind the open Dependabot alerts in both\nclients/ and docs/. Most moved within their parents'' existing semver ranges;\nnx and lerna pin smol-toml, brace-expansion and pacote exactly, so those need\nresolutions in clients/package.json.\n\nThe brace-expansion resolution is scoped to nx on purpose: unscoped, it also\ndrags the unaffected ^1.1.7 consumers across a major version for no security\nbenefit.\n\nimage-size remains vulnerable and unfixable here: 2.0.2 is the latest release\nand Docusaurus 3.10.2 still requires ^2.0.2.","tree":{"sha":"a7af57e04d63999be3b765c12d8deebe13730223","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/trees/a7af57e04d63999be3b765c12d8deebe13730223"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/prompt-edu/prompt/git/commits/32eead31b1e6feaa0c18772a4a6ff10ea5afe90a","verification":{"payload":"tree a7af57e04d63999be3b765c12d8deebe13730223\nparent bb7ca04b66d26d6c5cd5aa48abb0f7b7e157ce82\nauthor Maximilian Rapp <53191688+rappm@users.noreply.github.com> 1789034816 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789034816 +0100\n\nGeneral: Fix the open Dependabot and CodeQL security alerts (#2186)\n\n* General: Stop logging the database password on service startup\n\nThe assessment, team allocation and self team allocation services logged the\nfull Postgres DSN, which embeds DB_PASSWORD, so the credential appeared in\nplain text whenever the log level was debug. Core logged the same unsanitised\nDSN.\n\nApply the pattern the interview and example services already use: hoist the\nDB_* lookups to package-level variables and log only host, port, database,\nuser and sslmode, so the password no longer reaches a logging call.\n\n* Dependencies: Patch the vulnerable transitive npm dependencies\n\nUpgrade the transitive packages behind the open Dependabot alerts in both\nclients/ and docs/. Most moved within their parents'' existing semver ranges;\nnx and lerna pin smol-toml, brace-expansion and pacote exactly, so those need\nresolutions in clients/package.json.\n\nThe brace-expansion resolution is scoped to nx on purpose: unscoped, it also\ndrags the unaffected ^1.1.7 consumers across a major version for no security\nbenefit.\n\nimage-size remains vulnerable and unfixable here: 2.0.2 is the latest release\nand Docusaurus 3.10.2 still requires ^2.0.2.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqooFACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzCAQAE2NcSDh1zhmDZGvu14OBFiV\nKPGUbw3ti8/R65DaLcIY6RRZKcVrId5tgYtoOfg/gpsq3lnEdE